window.BloodDocs={"chronicle": [{"date": "The last five years", "html": "<h2>St. Michael's</h2>\n<p>The clinic closed at ten. That was the rule, and Claude kept it the way she kept all the rules\nthat were written down, which was mostly.</p>\n<p>She was stacking intake forms in the back of the van when he came up the sidewalk. Good coat.\nGood shoes. Not a man who needed a street clinic, and not a man who looked like he'd come to\nvolunteer, so she waited for him to say what he wanted.</p>\n<p>He wanted to talk about her research.</p>\n<p>He knew about it, which should have bothered her more than it did: how to treat people who never\ncame back to the same clinic twice, who were somewhere else by the time the results came in. He'd\nread it. He had questions, and they were good\nquestions, and somewhere around the third one she realized she'd stopped stacking forms.</p>\n<p>Then he asked her what happened to research like hers when the researcher died.</p>\n<p>She knew the answer. Everyone in the field knew it. Someone gets on a roll, a real one, the kind\nof streak you get once in a career, and then they get sick or they get hit by a bus, and the\nwhole thing stops. Their notes sit in a box. Somebody picks it up three years later and spends\ntwo of them working out what the first person already knew in their sleep. Momentum doesn't\ntransfer. You can't bequeath it.</p>\n<p>\"Unless,\" he said, \"you don't die.\"</p>\n<p>His name was Dominik Levesque. He talked for a long time about legacy, and discovery, and what a\nmind like hers could do if it simply kept going, fed the right resources, for as long as it\nwanted. It was a sales pitch. She knew it was a sales pitch. She was thirty years old and very good\nat her job, and she said yes.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>She woke in the dark.</p>\n<p>Not a room. Something tighter than a room, with walls that didn't give when she hit them, and she\nhit them for a long time. Hours. Maybe longer. Maybe more than one night; there was no way to\ntell, and no clock in her body anymore to tell it by. There was only the hunger, which was not\nlike being hungry. It was like being a hole.</p>\n<p>When the lid finally came off, it wasn't to let her out.</p>\n<p>He had brought something. She never learned what. It might not have been a person; she has\nthought about that more than she would like, and she still doesn't know. She fed on it until the\nscreaming in her head went quiet enough to hear him, and what she heard was disgust.</p>\n<p>She was the wrong thing. She understood that much before she understood anything else. He'd\nwanted a successor, someone to carry his work in the Blood forward, and what he'd made was thin.\nWeak. A mistake with a pulse she could fake.</p>\n<p>Toronto has rules about mistakes like her. Other cities are kinder; this one isn't. Nobody who\nholds a seat at the Prince's court may be thin-blooded, or may have made one. And a thin-blood\nwho meets the Final Death has not, in the eyes of the court, been murdered. Killing her would\ncost him nothing. Keeping her would cost him everything.</p>\n<p>He couldn't do it anyway.</p>\n<p>Whatever it was, cowardice or something that wanted to be mercy, he couldn't put her down\nhimself. So he walked her across the city with his hand on her arm, and he gave her to someone\ncalled Moth. They spoke for a minute, too low for her to follow. Something changed hands.</p>\n<p>She found out much later what it was, and what it had been meant to buy.</p>\n<p>She hasn't seen Dominik Levesque in five years.</p>\n<h2>The Basement</h2>\n<p>Olly found the building on his own, which was the whole point.</p>\n<p>Most of what he did ran on tips. Somebody knew somebody who'd been in the old bottling plant, or\nthe tunnel under the rail yards, and the spots moved through the scene like currency, and by the\ntime you got there, three other crews had already tagged the walls. This one was his. Nobody had\nposted it. Nobody had talked about it at any party he'd been to. He knew the signs of an empty\nbuilding the way other people knew their own street, and this one had all of them.</p>\n<p>He went down to the basement with his camera and the flash up. Pop. The dark jumped back and\nsettled again. Pop. That one came out good. He could already see the flyer.</p>\n<p>Then he stepped through a doorway and the place wasn't empty anymore.</p>\n<p>No cookware, which ruled out meth. No bed, which ruled out squatters. A table with papers on it.\nNotebooks, stacks of them, every page crammed edge to edge with tight little cursive, and\nsymbols in the margins: triangles with circles in them, planets maybe, like some astrology girl\nlived here. The writing was mostly English and then it wasn't, sliding into\nother alphabets and back again mid-sentence. He flipped to the back. The last entry was a night\nold. Two at most.</p>\n<p>Okay. Occupied. Not his.</p>\n<p>He turned to go and there was a man standing right behind him, close enough to touch, and the man\nopened his mouth and Olly saw the teeth.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>He woke up tilted.</p>\n<p>Strapped at the wrists and ankles to a board, the kind paramedics carry people down stairs on,\nbolted to a heavy wooden table that had been hinged so it could pivot. He was hanging at about\nforty-five degrees. His mouth tasted like pennies. And he was hungry in a way he had no word for.</p>\n<p>Olly had done a lot of drugs. He had never done this one.</p>\n<p>He started screaming. He screamed questions, and when the questions didn't work, he screamed\neverything else. The man at the other table kept working. Eventually he set down whatever he was\nholding, walked over, and slapped Olly across the face.</p>\n<p>Olly kept screaming. The man slapped him again. Not hard. Not angry. The way you'd tap a vending\nmachine. He kept doing it, patiently, until Olly understood that this was going to go on for\nexactly as long as the screaming did, and stopped.</p>\n<p>\"Usually,\" the man said, \"the penalty for interrupting my space is death.\" The accent was thick,\nEgyptian. The voice was very calm. \"But you could be very useful. Would you like to be useful, or\nwould you like to die?\"</p>\n<p>\"There's no law against taking pictures,\" Olly said. \"I was just taking pictures. Let me go.\nYou'll never see me again.\"</p>\n<p>\"You fail to understand.\" The man smiled, and there were the teeth again. \"I have drained you of\nyour blood. I have made you into one of my own kind. You will need to drink blood as well.\"</p>\n<p>He explained some things. Olly didn't take in most of them. Then the man pressed his own wrist to\nOlly's mouth, and for a second everything was very, very good, and then the wrist was gone and the\nhole in Olly was only a little smaller than before.</p>\n<p>The man's name was Rami. He had two conditions.</p>\n<p>The first was that he would feed on Olly. Carefully. Sustainably. That was the word he used.</p>\n<p>The second took longer to explain. Rami would put things into him, precursors, he called them, and\nOlly's body would do something to them over a week or two, and then Rami would take them out\nagain. A furnace, Rami said. A vessel for the work. And as long as Olly came when he was called and\ndid not interfere with the work, he was free to be his own man.</p>\n<p>What got to Olly, in the end, wasn't the vampire part. Vampires being real was fine. Vampires being\nreal was honestly kind of cool. What got to him was the rudeness of it. This was a guy who hung out\nunderground. Olly hung out underground. They should have been chill. He said so, more than once,\nwhile Rami talked right through him.</p>\n<p>But Rami never raised his voice. Never hurried. Somewhere in all that calm, Olly's heart stopped\npounding, and it was a while before he noticed he didn't have one to pound. He chose to be useful.\nRami undid the straps.</p>\n<h2>The Furnace</h2>\n<p>The name <em>Hollis</em> turned up in the first few months: in the margins of the notebooks, in things Rami\nsaid and then stopped saying. Whoever Hollis was, he'd been here before Olly.</p>\n<p>Olly learned the shape of it. Rami didn't deal in volume. He made one thing, more or less, the\nKindred answer to ecstasy, and he made it for customers he plainly despised. He never touched it\nhimself. To make it, he needed what he called an <em>athanor corporis</em>: the furnace of the body. A\nliving vessel to cook in.</p>\n<p>\"I want to cook something up,\" Rami would say, or \"I need to boil something,\" and that meant an IV.\nThree or four times a month, never more than an hour or two. Sometimes a bag went in and came out a\nweek later and a new one went in. Sometimes he got a week and a half off.</p>\n<p>Olly was never high, exactly. His body was doing the work, not enjoying it. But the work had\nweather. One batch left him wired for a week, ceiling-staring, talking a mile a minute to anyone\nwho'd stand still. Another dropped him into a hole so deep he couldn't find the edges. Rami taught\nhim about resonance, how blood carries the mood of whoever it came out of, and started sending\nhim out with instructions. <em>Find me sanguine blood this week. Something hot.</em> Olly got good at\nreading people. He got good at feeding against the swing, a downer for the up weeks, an upper for\nthe down, like a guy managing his own meds out of other people's arms.</p>\n<p>Sometimes Rami fed on him, and that was strange too. Olly knew what it was like for the people he\nfed on: not agony, not even close, more like a slow blissed-out drift. It turned out it was like\nthat for him too. When Rami fed, a warm flat calm came over him, the feeling of lying down in a\nreal bed after three days awake. <em>This is fine. This is okay.</em> It lingered for a while afterward.</p>\n<p>He didn't think much about the arrangement. That was the idea.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>He still threw parties. Of course he did.</p>\n<p>Olly's life before hadn't stopped because he'd died. The scene didn't know, and he wasn't going to\ntell it. He found spots. He talked himself up the sides of buildings he had no business climbing,\nbecause he'd said out loud that he would, and saying it out loud was how he made himself do\nthings. Once, in a tunnel with a curved arch and acoustics like a cathedral, he'd thrown the\nbiggest one he'd ever thrown: speakers stacked at one end, drum and bass loud enough to feel in\nyour fillings, and the word let all the way out, normies and all, because a spot like that you\nonly ever used once.</p>\n<p>Now the parties were how he ate.</p>\n<p>Two people helped. Dmitri was urbex, a mortal who knew where Olly's signal spots were and texted\nwhen he had something. What Dmitri had was usually a crypto guy. Some kid who'd made it big on a\ncoin with a dog on it and wanted the real thing now, the private tour, no posers, the best view\nin the city. Dmitri sold them the view. Olly met them at the top.</p>\n<p>Olly had rules about it, mostly. He went for people who had it coming, or near enough: the frat\nboys, the Bitcoin bros, the guys who wandered into the scene like it owed them something. People\nwho needed taking down a notch.</p>\n<p>The other one was Trieste.</p>\n<p>He'd met her at a rave. She could walk into any bar in the city and walk out with the worst man in\nit, and she'd bring him somewhere abandoned, and there Olly would be. She had a crew she ran with,\nhalf a dozen of them. She'd asked Olly along more than once, and he'd always found a reason not to\ngo.</p>\n<p>One night they'd holed up together in one of his spots. She stretched when the sun went down and\nsaid she had something to show him, and there on a mirror were two lines of cocaine.</p>\n<p>Black.</p>\n<p>She did one and handed him the bill. He had it halfway to his nose before he stopped. \"What <em>is</em>\nthis?\"</p>\n<p>She looked at him like he'd asked something weird. \"This was Jonah.\"</p>\n<p>Jonah. He knew the name. One of the crew.</p>\n<p>\"What did you do to Jonah?\"</p>\n<p>\"I didn't <em>do</em> anything.\" She shrugged. \"It was sunrise. This is what we do with them after. I cut\nsome L into it.\" L was Rami's. Olly knew that much. L was the thing Olly was the furnace for. \"It's\na good way to remember someone.\"</p>\n<p>He did the line.</p>\n<p>It was the best he'd felt since he died.</p>\n<p>Later, she told him about it. Once a month, she and her crew went somewhere with a view of the\neast and sat out and watched the sun come up, and waited to see who didn't quite make it back\ninside in time. She knew somebody who could brew something that helped with the burns, if he was\nnervous.</p>\n<p>\"You guys are into some weird shit,\" Olly said.</p>\n<p>\"It's a bonding activity.\"</p>\n<p>\"Jonah,\" he said. \"I won't forget him.\"</p>\n<p>\"See?\" said Trieste. \"It works.\"</p>\n<p>He told her he'd come. Not next month. He had an event coming up. But after that, sure, for real.\nHe'd said it out loud now. That was how he made himself do things.</p>\n<h2>Stash Number Three</h2>\n<p>Moth never stayed anywhere longer than seven nights.</p>\n<p>It was the curse of Moth's line, Moth explained, the way someone else might explain a bad knee.\nStay put past the seventh night and the Blood turns on you. So Moth kept a loop: storage units,\nwarehouses, crates of old museum pieces, and a dozen little troves in between, each stocked with\nwhatever Moth had gathered. Books. Bones. Things in jars. Moth was a magpie with a\nlibrary card. And every few nights they were on the move again, and sometimes the move went\nsideways: <em>Not that one. We'll skip that one for now. Actually, I need the thing in stash number\nthree.</em></p>\n<p>Moth looked about thirty and was probably ten years older than that. Claude and Moth were not\nfriends, exactly. Claude respected Moth, and she\nwasn't sure yet that she trusted Moth, and Moth seemed to find both of those things correct. It\nwas closer to fostering: every new safehouse came with a new book, and every book came with a quiz.</p>\n<p>The book Moth kept coming back to was a bad one.</p>\n<p><em>Le Livre Antique du Sang</em>: the Ancient Book of Blood, rendered from Persian into French by\nPéladan, the occultist, who had apparently not been much of a translator. Claude's French wasn't\nmuch better. Between the two of them they worked at it for years, and it gave up its meanings\nslowly. It described a process. A series of transformations the author called <em>gates</em>.</p>\n<p>Moth read. Claude tried things.</p>\n<p>That was the difference, and it was why it worked. Moth collected; Moth didn't <em>do</em>. Claude was a\nresearcher, and a researcher who reads a procedure wants to run it. Her first attempts failed, badly\nand instructively. She learned what equipment she needed, and what she was missing, and Moth\nstarted buying ingredients alongside the artifacts. Through Moth's dealers, and their dealers,\nClaude caught the edges of something she hadn't known was there: a scattered, unofficial community\nacross Toronto of people doing strange things with blood. No meetups. No names. Just a referral\nhere, a supplier's other buyer mentioned there.</p>\n<p>One night Moth set out everything for the next chapter before Claude had even reached it. There was\none jar Claude didn't recognize.</p>\n<p>\"The ashes of a vampire,\" Moth said. \"That one was hard to find.\"</p>\n<p>\"What did you trade for it?\"</p>\n<p>\"It's fine. Someone owed me a boon. I forgave it.\"</p>\n<p>Claude knew enough by then to know what a boon was: a favour, owed by something that would not\ndie, that could be called in a century later. Currency, of a kind nobody printed. She thought about\nasking where the ashes had come from.</p>\n<p>She didn't. She was too excited to run the experiment.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>The next safehouse in the loop had been torn apart.</p>\n<p>Not robbed. Searched. Drawers out, boxes slit, the kind of mess people make when they're looking for\none specific thing and don't find it. They didn't stay. They went on to the next spot on the list,\nand went in together, circling the room in opposite directions. Nothing out of place. Moth set a\nlittle alarm at the door, just in case, and they settled in.</p>\n<p>That was the night Moth told her the truth.</p>\n<p>Over the past months Moth had been explaining where things came from, who sold what and for how\nmuch, because Claude would need to know eventually. And so that night Moth told her about the book.\nAbout how it had come to Moth: payment, from a Tremere named Dominik Levesque, for a job. The job\nwas her.</p>\n<p>Moth hadn't done it, obviously. And Moth was fairly sure Dominik had thought the book was worthless\nwhen he handed it over.</p>\n<p>Dominik was close with the Prince, Moth said. His library was supposed to be extraordinary. Good\nluck ever seeing it.</p>\n<p>Then Moth told her something more useful. The process in the book that had been stalling her for\nweeks: Moth was fairly sure, now, what it was describing. And there was a supplier who\nhad the equipment for it, and who might lend it. His name was Rami.</p>\n<h2>Daylight</h2>\n<p>The alarm didn't go off.</p>\n<p>Claude woke anyway, and the wrongness of it hit her before anything else. The sun was still up. She\ncould feel it through the earth, a weight on her body like wet sand. And there were people at the\ndoor. More than one.</p>\n<p>She crawled to Moth. She shook Moth. She said Moth's name, then shouted it. Nothing. Moth was down\nin the day the way the dead are down.</p>\n<p>The door came in.</p>\n<p>Three of them, maybe four. Tactical gear, bug-eyed helmets, rifles. Lights so bright they turned the\nroom white. Claude went behind whatever was nearest and made herself small. She was not going to\nleave Moth.</p>\n<p>One of them walked over to Moth where Moth lay.</p>\n<p><em>Pop. Pop.</em></p>\n<p>Claude didn't decide anything after that.</p>\n<p>There was a strength in her that she had never felt and didn't know she had. She came out of hiding\nand took the first one's arm off at the shoulder. It came away clean. The second one she caught by\nthe throat, and then she didn't have a throat in her hand so much as the inside of one. The third\ngot his rifle around toward her and she took it out of his hands and drove the stock back into his\nface.</p>\n<p>She was on top of one of them and drinking before she knew it. She had never killed anyone. She'd\nbuilt a whole way of living around making sure she never would. The thing that had come out of hiding\ndidn't care about any of that.</p>\n<p>She stopped anyway. She doesn't know how. She pulled her mouth off him while there was still\nsomething left in him.</p>\n<p>Then she went to Moth.</p>\n<p>One of them was dead, and not from feeding. Two were down and bleeding. One had run. She dug the\nbullets out of Moth with whatever she had, and then she looked at the two who were still breathing\nand thought about consent, and how the question had stopped applying the moment they came through\nthe door. She drained one of them nearly dry, into Moth, so that Moth could lie still and heal and\nnot have to hunt for it.</p>\n<p>The other she left where he lay. They took what they could carry and went somewhere new, somewhere\nthat wasn't on the loop at all, and they didn't go back.</p>\n<h2>The Curse</h2>\n<p>Something went wrong with Rami.</p>\n<p>It started like any feeding. The warm, flat calm; the bed after three days awake. Olly was halfway\nasleep in it when he noticed, the way you notice an alarm going off in someone else's apartment. It\nhad gone on longer than it usually did.</p>\n<p>\"Hey,\" he said. \"Rami.\"</p>\n<p>Rami grunted. Heard him. Didn't stop.</p>\n<p>It came on like swimming too deep: the slow realization that the surface is further than you\nthought, then the fast one, that you may not make it. Olly started to struggle. Rami held on. They\nwent off the chair together, locked up, stumbling across the lab in a clinch with Rami's mouth still\nat his neck and Olly's strength going out of him in pulls.</p>\n<p>His bag was hanging on the corner of a shelf.</p>\n<p>He'd always carried. Nobody had ever asked him why, which was good, because the answer was mostly\n<em>because</em>. He dragged the two of them over to the bag. He got a hand in. He got a hand on the\nholster, and then on the grip, and he put three rounds into Rami's stomach at a distance of no\ninches at all.</p>\n<p>Rami let go.</p>\n<p>He reeled back and dropped into the chair Olly had been sitting in and stayed there, holding\nhimself. He didn't get up. He didn't come at Olly again.</p>\n<p>Olly paced. He had trusted this guy. That was what his brain kept circling back to, while his hands\nwent looking for something to tie with, because tying Rami down seemed like the kind of thing a\nperson should do in this situation, even if Olly wasn't sure it would help. He found straps. He got\nRami's wrists fixed to the chair.</p>\n<p>Then he stepped back and watched Rami heal.</p>\n<p>Rami breathed deep and slow, like someone concentrating very hard, and the holes in him closed. It\nwas not a fast process, but it was one Olly could see happening, flesh drawing back in on itself\nlike film run backwards. When it was done, Rami's eyes opened, and he sat forward against the straps.</p>\n<p>\"The curse got me,\" he said. \"I am sorry for having endangered you.\"</p>\n<p>\"What do you mean? Were you <em>planning</em> that?\"</p>\n<p>\"No. You are of such use to me. I would never endanger you willingly.\"</p>\n<p>Olly stared at him. \"Is this what happened to the last guy?\"</p>\n<p>\"No.\" Something crossed Rami's face. \"Hollis was a fool. He insulted the Prince.\" He let his head\nfall back. \"I am Banu Haqim. My sire's sire's sire, back and back — it is our curse. When we feed\nupon the blood of our own kind, sometimes we cannot stop.\" He looked at Olly. \"This may be your\ncurse as well. But unlike me, you do not need the blood of our kind to live.\"</p>\n<p>\"It's real convenient that you feed off me, then.\" Olly's voice came out high. \"Look. It's been\ngood. This has been going great. But I don't want that to happen again.\"</p>\n<p>Rami didn't answer. He only let his eyes close, as if to say <em>another night</em>.</p>\n<p>Olly undid the straps. \"Shooting you didn't seem to do too much,\" he said. \"You need anything? Are\nyou going to be okay?\"</p>\n<p>Rami had fed enough, he said. More than enough. He leaned back against the stretcher-board and\ndidn't move.</p>\n<p>Olly got his bag. On the way out, without turning around, he mumbled, \"Sorry I shot you.\"</p>\n<h2>The Gate of Flesh</h2>\n<p>A few nights later, Moth took Claude to meet Rami.</p>\n<p>He'd gotten Moth's messages. He had exactly what she needed. And it was good timing, he said; he\nwould send her his boy. Olly would come and help with her project, and when she was finished, Moth\ncould consider the favour between them paid, and Olly could come home. He pressed his thumb into a\nsmall token and gave it to her. Olly would know what it meant.</p>\n<p>She went alone.</p>\n<p>Olly was sitting outside one of the houses he crashed at, smoking. He didn't get up.</p>\n<p>\"I think we're supposed to meet?\" Claude said.</p>\n<p>\"There's no party tonight.\"</p>\n<p>\"No, no. Rami sent me. You're supposed to help me.\"</p>\n<p>He took the token and looked at it and then looked at her properly for the first time. \"You saw\nRami? How's he doing?\"</p>\n<p>\"He seemed all right. Kind of a weird guy.\"</p>\n<p>\"Yeah.\" Olly laughed once, not really a laugh. \"What's your name?\"</p>\n<p>\"Claudette. But you can call me Claude.\"</p>\n<p>\"Okay. I'm Olly. What am I helping you with?\"</p>\n<p>She glanced at the house. \"Are there people around?\"</p>\n<p>\"In the house. They're cool.\" He considered her. \"Maybe let's go for a walk.\"</p>\n<p>They walked. She talked, and it was too much and too fast and pitched too high for someone who'd\nnever read a page of it, and she knew it and couldn't stop. The book. The process. How the book said\nshe needed an athanor to make it do what it promised. How apparently he was one.</p>\n<p>\"Yeah,\" Olly said. \"Rami uses me as some kind of drug vial for whatever he's cooking.\"</p>\n<p>The book called the process a series of gates, she told him. And the first gate was the Gate of Flesh.</p>\n<p>\"I'm the Gate of Flesh.\"</p>\n<p>\"Yes.\"</p>\n<p>\"Jesus.\" He smoked. \"Okay.\"</p>\n<p>\"If that's all right.\"</p>\n<p>\"I never get a say in this.\"</p>\n<p>\"No, it's really important that you get a say.\" She stopped walking. \"Rami gave you to Moth, my\nteacher, as payment for a favour. I know how that sounds. But if it hurts, or anything, we don't have\nto do it again. Just once. I just need to see if it works.\"</p>\n<p>\"And what are you getting out of this?\"</p>\n<p>\"Knowledge.\"</p>\n<p>\"What am I getting out of this?\"</p>\n<p>She didn't have an answer ready, so she asked him instead. \"What do you want?\"</p>\n<p>He thought about it for a while. \"What's the next gate?\"</p>\n<p>\"The Gate of Names.\"</p>\n<p>Olly flicked the end of his cigarette into the street. \"When you get to the Gate of Names,\" he said,\n\"can I come too?\"</p>\n<p>\"Yeah,\" said Claude. \"Yeah.\"</p>", "part": "One", "played": "2025-06-24", "slug": "01-the-gate-of-flesh", "title": "The Gate of Flesh", "words": 4452}, {"html": "<h2>Muscle</h2>\n<p>Ethan Cole had spent his whole life about one bad week away from the other side of the counter.</p>\n<p>Where he grew up there were two careers on offer: the law, or the thing the law was for. He'd\npicked the one that paid on Fridays. Bail bonds. A man had bailed him out once, literally, cash on\nthe table at two in the morning, and then kept him around afterwards and taught him the trade,\nand that was as close to a second chance as anyone on his street ever got. Night shifts were no\nhardship. Bail doesn't keep office hours. Nobody at work noticed when Ethan stopped needing the\ndays.</p>\n<p>Rose had made him. She was Brujah, she worked for the Baron of Kensington, and every so often she\ndropped a word nobody had used in a century. Ethan was pretty sure that meant she was ancient. He wouldn't have known the difference\nbetween the nineteen-twenties and the sixteen-hundreds if you'd paid him; old was old, and she had\nstories about all of it.</p>\n<p>She'd been good to him, was the thing. She'd wanted him. Not what he'd turned out to be, exactly;\nthe blood in him was thin, and they both knew it. But she was teaching him anyway. The Traditions.\nThe players. Who you tipped your hat to if you were crossing east through the Prince's ground, and\nwho you didn't cross at all. He was the runt, but he could hold his own. He could catch up.</p>\n<p>Tonight she stopped on a street in Kensington and pointed.</p>\n<p>A two-storey house, boarded windows, a dump. Greek letters still on the front, half of them fallen\noff. An old frat house, or what was left of one.</p>\n<p>\"So,\" Rose said. \"You ready to be my muscle tonight?\"</p>\n<p>\"Sure,\" Ethan said. \"I guess. That's what I do.\"</p>\n<p>The Baron, Evelyn, liked people who played with blood to do it carefully, Rose explained. In a\ncoordinated way. In a way that did not bring the Prince down on Kensington, or his Sheriff, or\nCSIS. There was an operation in the basement of this house that had been told to move and hadn't.\nThey were here to check it was running safe.</p>\n<p>Ethan didn't ask what <em>check</em> meant. It was pretty clear.</p>\n<h2>Sneakernet</h2>\n<p>The week's batch had Olly wired.</p>\n<p>Whatever Rami had in him this time came on like three espressos and a good idea, all the time, for\ndays. He'd been taking every shortcut in the city at a jog, talking too much, going down streets he\nhad no business on. If somebody had tried to stick him up that week, Olly would have high-fived him\non the way past and kept going, and left him standing there with his hand up.</p>\n<p>Which was fine, because the Pony Express paid for legs, not judgment.</p>\n<p>It turned out Kindred who didn't trust phones still needed to move data, and the fastest way to\nmove a lot of data across a city is still a man with a bag. Olly had a duffel full of hard drives\nover his shoulder and a new address in Kensington, and the address turned out to be a half-condemned\nfrat house with a light on the ground floor and something chemical rising out of the basement.</p>\n<p>Bleach. And under the bleach, something worse.</p>\n<p>Down the stairs it was a lab. A real one, or near enough. At the far end, a row of desktop towers\nwired together and grinding through something. A centrifuge on a bench that had definitely lived in\na hospital until recently. Pots, beakers, a guy hunched over a keyboard hammering out code. Three or\nfour people, working hard, not looking up.</p>\n<p>One of them was at the centrifuge. Olly knew him.</p>\n<p>\"Todd?\" He was grinning before he'd decided to. \"Todd, right?\"</p>\n<p>Todd looked. Looked again. \"Holy shit. What the fuck are you doing here?\"</p>\n<p>\"Bringing you hard drives, I guess.\"</p>\n<p>\"I didn't know you were also— It's been a crazy year for me.\" Todd pulled his lip aside with a thumb\nand showed a fang. \"Did they ghoul you, or are you a biter?\"</p>\n<p>\"Oh, fuck, that's awesome.\" Olly grinned wider. \"Yeah. They've got me going all around town. Guess we\nwon't be throwing any parties at this house.\"</p>\n<p>\"Not today, that's for sure.\"</p>\n<p>They didn't have anything fresh, Todd said, but there were some interesting blends in the fridge if\nOlly wanted to try one. How into weed had he been, back in the day? Because they'd worked out\nsomething like weed, in blood, in a bottle. Bottom shelf, on the left.</p>\n<p>It was carbonated. Todd told him not to chug it. Olly chugged it, and went and sat in a chair in the\ncorner, and for a while he thought about alchemy, about the whole idea of it, of turning one thing\ninto another thing, of being the vessel where that happened. It all seemed very profound. The\ntowers hummed. He watched the room the way you watch a movie you've seen before.</p>\n<h2>You. Sit Down.</h2>\n<p>Rose went down the stairs first and came up against the operation like weather.</p>\n<p>Ethan had never seen her like this. The easy talk was gone. She walked straight to a woman at the\nbench, got in her face, and started taking her apart: <em>I thought we talked about this. What is the\ndeal here?</em></p>\n<p>The woman's name was Jaren, and she didn't back up an inch. \"We already moved twice. We're not\nmoving again. You can tell Evelyn to shove it. We're not causing any problems here.\"</p>\n<p>It went up from there. Somebody else, Todd, got to his feet with his hands out, trying to calm it\ndown. Then Jaren put both hands on Rose and shoved her.</p>\n<p>Ethan didn't think about it. He pointed at the one who'd stood up. \"You. Sit down.\"</p>\n<p>He said it the way he said things to clients who were about to make his night longer, and Todd sat.\nMeekly. Pulled his chair back in like a kid in class.</p>\n<p>Ethan went for Jaren. He didn't swing. He just put his shoulder into her and kept walking, and she\nwent backwards until there was nowhere left to go. Then he stood there and looked big. He was good\nat looking big.</p>\n<p>Rose straightened her coat. \"Unplug those,\" she said, pointing at the computers. \"We're taking\nwhatever they are somewhere secure. This operation is closed.\"</p>\n<p>Ethan went for the towers. Not the monitors, which were junk. The boxes with the brains in them.\nFour desktops and a duffel bag full of hard drives somebody had dropped by the door.</p>\n<p>Behind him, Rose was doing something to the room. Ethan felt the edge of it, a pressure, like\nstanding near a speaker with the bass turned all the way up and no sound coming out. The thin-bloods\nfelt the whole thing. They sat where they were and watched their work get carried up the stairs.\nNobody tried anything.</p>\n<p>In the corner, Olly watched too, from inside the weed-blood, and started to feel like this was his\nproblem.</p>\n<p>These were his people, sort of. Todd was his friend, sort of. The delivery he'd just made was going\nup the stairs in the arms of a guy he didn't know. And there was a pistol in his backpack, and the\nbackpack was on his lap, and he got his hand inside it very slowly, so nobody would notice. The gun\nseemed to keep moving away from his fingers. The whole room had gone paranoid.</p>\n<p>Todd had a single tear of blood running down one cheek. He wasn't wiping it off.</p>\n<p>Olly stood up. He walked over to Todd with the gun in his hand, down at his side, because he had\nforgotten it was there.</p>\n<p>\"Todd,\" he whispered. \"What's going on? Who are these people? Why are they taking the servers?\"</p>\n<p>Rose turned and leaned on him, the same way she was leaning on everyone else. He felt it. He felt the\nroom wanting him to look at her, and be quiet, and sit. But whatever Rami had cooking in him this\nweek was louder than Rose. He stayed on Todd.</p>\n<p>\"I don't know, man.\" Todd was stammering. \"Evelyn keeps shutting us down. We're trying to follow the\nrules. I don't know what the deal is.\"</p>\n<h2>Fire</h2>\n<p>Ethan was coming back down the stairs for the lab equipment when he saw it all at once.</p>\n<p>The guy with the gun, talking to Todd. Rose, turned toward them, distracted. Jaren, edging closer to\nTodd. And the third one, the one nobody had been watching, tipping back a metal flask and drinking the\nwhole thing down, walking up behind Rose while he did it.</p>\n<p>Ethan moved before he had a plan. He put himself between the flask and Rose's back, which was the\nwhole plan. Be the wall. Find out what's happening. React.</p>\n<p>The guy spat.</p>\n<p>It came out of him as fire. There was no lighter, no spark, nothing to catch. He just breathed out\nand the breath was burning, and it hit Ethan square in the face.</p>\n<p>Something at the very bottom of Ethan started screaming. The oldest thing in him. The thing that\nknows what fire does to the dead. He heard it, and he muted it, and he kept his feet. He was on fire\nand he knew it and he dropped anyway, because that's what you do: stop, drop, roll. He heard Rose\ngrabbing for something to beat him out with.</p>\n<p>Olly didn't have that kind of control.</p>\n<p>The fire went up and so did every thought in his head. There was only the door. The door was up the\nstairs, and the stairs were past Rose and past the man rolling on the floor, and Olly went over him,\nliterally over him, and up and out into the street and kept going, and did not once look back to see\nif anyone was behind him.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>When Ethan could think again, he was lying on the basement floor, smoking, and it was very quiet.</p>\n<p>He hurt. He'd started to get used to how little things hurt now, bruises and cuts that just closed.\nThis hurt worse than he remembered burns hurting when he was alive. He lay there and let the scream at\nthe bottom of him settle, and then he looked around.</p>\n<p>Rose was standing. Rose was fine.</p>\n<p>Nobody else in the basement was alive. Three of them were still there, on the floor or slumped at the\nbenches, not burned, not torn, not marked in any way he could see. Just stopped. Their faces had\nfrozen in the middle of something, and the something was horror.</p>\n<p>The one who'd breathed fire wasn't among them.</p>\n<p>\"Can you walk?\" Rose said. \"Do you need help getting out?\"</p>\n<p>\"I can walk.\" He got up. \"But that fucking hurt.\"</p>\n<p>\"Fire's nothing to mess around with.\" She was looking at the bodies, and then at the empty flask on\nthe floor, with a different kind of interest. \"I'm very intrigued by that flask. We'll take everything.\nAll of it.\" She turned back to him. \"Did you see who ran out?\"</p>\n<p>\"Honestly? I barely noticed him.\"</p>\n<p>\"Then that's your job, once you've rested. A loose thread. Find him.\"</p>\n<h2>Sandman</h2>\n<p>Ralph Begg was handsome, and he'd been handsome his whole life, and it had gotten him about as far as\nhandsome gets you.</p>\n<p>He'd been a jock in high school who couldn't quite make it at the next level. After that, a string of\ndot-com jobs he was good at right up until someone noticed what else he was doing with the access.\nThen a con. Then a job. Then a con. When California stopped being any fun he came home to Toronto and\nmoved into the basement of an old high-school friend's house in the Junction Triangle. The friend was\none of the few people Ralph had never taken advantage of. He'd decided to keep it that way.</p>\n<p>Then he found a way to skim paychecks out of a gig-economy app, and the app turned out to belong to\nsomething that was not a person.</p>\n<p>He never saw it coming. He only saw it at the end: bald, big ears, too many teeth, like Count Orlok\nout of the old silent movie. It had picked him for a reason, he gathered later, a pretty boy with\ncomputer skills, something it could use and make ugly. Except Ralph didn't get ugly. Whatever it gave\nhim didn't take right. It got angry about that, and it broke his neck and left him in the gutter.</p>\n<p>He woke up the next night with his head on backwards.</p>\n<p>It turned around, eventually. Most things did. Then he spent three weeks working out what he was, the\nway he'd worked out everything else in his life: alone, by trial and error, with a laptop. The sun hurt.\nFood came back up. People didn't seem to mind very much if you drank from them, as long as you didn't\nwake them. And somebody, some time in the last ten years, had very carefully scrubbed every word about\ncreatures like him off the internet. The holes were still there if you knew how to look for holes.\nRalph knew how to look for holes. That was when he got scared.</p>\n<p>He fed like a sandman. Middle-class streets, quiet houses, back doors that stuck but opened. A nice\nathleisure set so he looked like a guy out for a jog at three in the morning, which people will\naccept. In and out. Nobody woke up.</p>\n<p>He was coming home from one of those nights, cutting through an alley, when he saw a couple kissing up\nagainst a fence.</p>\n<p>It was only the angle. The streetlight caught their silhouette exactly right. And then it wasn't a kiss\nanymore. It was feeding, and it was vicious, and for the first time in his life he was seeing it done by\nsomeone who wasn't him.</p>\n<p>He should have kept walking. He didn't. He went quiet instead, a trick he'd found he could do, making\nhimself into a gap in the night where no sound came from. He went down the far side of the alley,\nthrough the shadows, until he was fifteen feet away.</p>\n<p>Two men. One tall and thin, six-four at least, in an old-fashioned suit. In his right hand, the hand\nnearest Ralph, he held something long and pale. The other one was shorter, younger, in a stage magician's\ncostume, and he was pinned against the fence and struggling, and the struggling was getting weaker.</p>\n<p>Ralph didn't feed until the kill. He'd never let it get close.</p>\n<p>He let the quiet go and kicked over a garbage can.</p>\n<p>The tall one swung around, and he did it without letting go, like a dancer dipping his partner, his\nmouth still locked on the other man's throat, and his eyes found Ralph's.</p>\n<p>The victim's mouth was open, and there were fangs in it. It was one of them, feeding on another one of\nthem. And the thing in the tall man's hand caught the light, and Ralph saw it was a bone, a long one, a\nfemur, and it was carved all the way up and down.</p>\n<p>\"Sorry,\" Ralph said. \"Didn't mean to interrupt. I was just going through the alley here. Sorry.\"</p>\n<p>Something came into his head then, from outside, and pushed.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Some time later, he found himself walking. Different street. Same neighbourhood. The sky had changed\ncolour in the east.</p>\n<p>He should have run for home. It wasn't far. He went back to the alley instead, because he had been\ncareful for three weeks and he was done with careful. He had to know.</p>\n<p>Blood on the gravel. A trail of it, going nowhere he could follow. No body. No tall man. But behind the\noverturned can, where it had been thrown and forgotten, the femur.</p>\n<p>He picked it up. It looked like something out of a museum, the kind of thing a colonizer stole from\nsomewhere a hundred years ago. The writing on it wasn't anything he could read.</p>\n<p>Then, because nobody was watching and he was who he was, he touched two fingers to the blood on the\ngravel and tasted it.</p>\n<p>It wasn't like the blood he knew. If that was savoury, this was something else: deeper, stranger. Umami.\nIt lit up something in him that nothing had lit before.</p>\n<p>He put the bone inside his Patagonia vest and jogged home.</p>\n<p>His roommate was up, finishing a beer at the kitchen table. \"Meet any girls out jogging?\"</p>\n<p>\"Tough night,\" Ralph said. \"Just here for the cardio.\"</p>\n<p>He went down to the room at the back of the basement, the one with no windows, and locked the door,\nand slept like the dead, which was what he was.</p>\n<h2>Methuselah's Ossuary</h2>\n<p>He didn't carry the bone at first. You don't walk around Toronto with a human femur. He put it in the\nsafe and took pictures instead, high resolution, every inch of it. He printed himself a student card for\nYork University, pulled on a hoodie, and tailgated a real student through the\nlibrary door.</p>\n<p>Anthropology. Ancient scripts. Then more ancient scripts.</p>\n<p>The writing, he worked out, was cuneiform. Or something descended from it: the same wedges and marks,\nnot as old as the Babylonian tablets in the books, but uncanny how close. Some of the characters verged\non drawings. That gave him a second question, and the second question took him further. Over the\nnights that followed he stopped doing anything else.</p>\n<p>He couldn't do it alone, so he didn't. He wrote to experts on the other side of the world, a professor\nof ancient languages in Mumbai, nobody local, from accounts he'd built for the purpose, and he read\neverything they sent back.</p>\n<p>A phrase kept turning up. <em>Methuselah's ossuary.</em> Bones like his bone. Treated as deeply magical,\nturning up in charnel houses and old collections all over the world. None of them as old as Babylon,\nwhatever the dealers claimed. He found references to four or five. One in Syria. One in Greece. One in\nChile. One in Singapore. And one that was plainly a fake, carved in a language somebody had made up\nafter 1950.</p>\n<p>By the third night he'd stopped leaving the bone in the safe. He carried it everywhere.</p>\n<p>Then two messages came in, to an account nobody should have been able to find.</p>\n<p><em>I have an interested buyer if you're selling.</em></p>\n<p>He blocked that one.</p>\n<p><em>Do you want to know what it says?</em></p>\n<p>He answered that one.</p>\n<p>They wrote back the same night. <em>Where would you like to meet?</em></p>\n<p>He'd never said where he was. They knew anyway, and closely. That terrified him. It also made the decision\nfor him: he had to learn. And he was a vampire now, wasn't he? He must be able to take care of himself.\nHe picked somewhere public: the Commons, the park by York. He put a fat envelope of cash in his pocket,\nthought about a gun, and decided against it.</p>\n<p>He wore a salmon-coloured polo shirt so they'd know him.</p>\n<h2>A Long-Lost Cousin</h2>\n<p>Ethan's burns had healed, mostly. He was hungry, but he was all right. Rose had come to get him with\nanother job.</p>\n<p>\"I'm not sure about the person we're meeting,\" she said, as they crossed the grass toward the lights.\n\"They have something much more valuable than they know. I'm trying to work out how stupid they are, or\nhow corrupt.\"</p>\n<p>\"Bring me along in case they're very stupid or very corrupt?\"</p>\n<p>\"That's our guy.\" She pointed at the salmon polo. \"Go and introduce yourself. Get a read on him. Come\nback when you're done.\"</p>\n<p>Ethan went over. The guy was standing with his hands in his pockets, radiating confidence at nobody.</p>\n<p>\"My boss sent me,\" Ethan said. \"You have the bone?\"</p>\n<p>\"Not on me. We're here to talk about it, not hand anything over.\" Friendly. Easy. \"It's in a safe place.\"</p>\n<p>\"Fair enough.\"</p>\n<p>\"We're strangers. Let's not be. I'm Ralph.\"</p>\n<p>\"Ethan.\"</p>\n<p>\"Nice to meet you, Ethan.\"</p>\n<p>So how had Ralph found it? A blind auction, Ralph said. A storage unit, packed in with some less\nimpressive antiques. No, he didn't know who'd owned it; with blind storage auctions they legally can't\ntell you. He did it every now and then. You could flip some money if you were careful: watches, bonds,\nold guns. Though ever since <em>Storage Wars</em> there were too many buyers and the margins weren't what they\nused to be.</p>\n<p>It was a good story. It was a very smooth story. Ethan couldn't tell Ralph wasn't human; Ralph looked\nwarm, and breathed, and his heart was going like anybody's.</p>\n<p>He walked back to Rose. \"Well, he's not stupid. I don't know about corrupt. Not saying he is. Just\ndon't think he's dumb enough to try anything.\"</p>\n<p>Rose went over herself.</p>\n<p>\"I hear your name's Ralph. I'm Rose.\" She smiled. \"Quite the find you have. What are your hopes for it?\"</p>\n<p>Ralph said he had higher-resolution photographs than the ones he'd shared, if she'd like to see them.\nShe'd implied she could tell him what it was.</p>\n<p>\"If it's real, and not a replica, it's a ritual object. For the dead, or for doing things to the dead.\nThat's typical of things like this.\" She opened a laptop that folded back into a tablet, and flicked\nthrough images of the bone that someone had marked up with a digital pen. Repeating shapes, circled.\n\"I'm not fluent. But this is a word for <em>horse</em>. This one is <em>lift</em>, or <em>threshold</em>. I don't have it all\nyet, and it isn't a one-person job. The people I'm working with want to know it's genuine. And they want\nto know it's safe.\"</p>\n<p>Ralph did his best. He wasn't selling anything. He'd confirmed it was real human bone, that the carving\nwas as old as the bone, not added later. He could share the scans. If they could work together to find\nout where it came from, he'd even let her handle it, somewhere controlled. But what was <em>her</em> interest?</p>\n<p>The pressure came then, and it was not like sleep, though it was the closest thing he had to compare it\nto. It was his words being nudged, gently and firmly, in a direction that wasn't his.</p>\n<p>Rose looked at him strangely. Then she reached out, took his chin in her hand, and lifted his lip with\nher thumb.</p>\n<p>\"Who's your sire?\"</p>\n<p>\"My—father?\"</p>\n<p>\"Who made you like this?\"</p>\n<p>He felt himself answer. \"He looked like Count Orlok. Have you ever seen <em>Nosferatu</em>? Bald, big ears,\ntoo many teeth.\"</p>\n<p>Rose turned to Ethan, delighted. \"I think we've found ourselves a long-lost cousin.\"</p>\n<p>Ethan stood up straighter. This wasn't some human he could put in the ground if things went wrong.\nThis was one of them, and he had no idea what it could do.</p>\n<p>Rose went on with the questions. Who did Ralph work with? <em>Himself.</em> Did he know who his Prince was?\nRalph had never heard the word used like that. She rattled off names, the Prince, Baron Evelyn, a few\nothers, and none of them meant anything to him. She laughed out loud.</p>\n<p>\"Well, isn't it our great fortune that we found you before you were torn to shreds.\" She let go of his\nchin. \"We need to get you, and that bone, somewhere safe before somebody comes hunting. Do you have a\nsecure place?\"</p>\n<p>He did, he said. But there was a roommate, and the roommate was in the dark, and Ralph was keeping him\nthere. He'd worked out on his own that you don't tell anyone. The feds were looking for anybody who said\nthe word.</p>\n<p>Rose looked impressed, and Ralph understood she thought he was keeping his food close to home. He didn't\ncorrect her.</p>\n<p>Ethan had somewhere. His office. It got some foot traffic in the day, but it had bars on the doors,\nbecause now and then a client needed holding. It wasn't much. But Ralph could stay there, for now.</p>\n<p>\"I'll leave the door unlocked,\" Ethan said. \"Which will be a first for me.\"</p>\n<p>And the bone? Rose asked. Would Ralph like it stored somewhere else? Somewhere she knew was secure?</p>\n<p>\"The bone is safe,\" Ralph said. \"I can get to it when I need it.\"</p>\n<p>Not the answer she wanted. She let it go. She tilted her head at Ethan, and they stepped outside.</p>\n<p>\"If he goes anywhere,\" Rose said quietly, \"follow him. If he spends the night here, go and search his\nhouse.\"</p>\n<h2>A Minor Boon</h2>\n<p>Olly went to Rami, after, because there wasn't anyone else to go to.</p>\n<p>He told him all of it. The frat house, the drop-off. How there'd seemed to be a chain of command that\nnobody had told him about, and his delivery had gone up the stairs in some stranger's arms. Whether any\nof that was <em>allowed</em>. And the guy with the flask. The fire.</p>\n<p>Rami listened soberly. The fire interested him; Olly could see it turning over behind his eyes. But he\nset it aside.</p>\n<p>\"It is not ideal,\" he said. \"There are processes we can pull on. But I have something more urgent for\nyou. I will owe you a minor boon for it.\" He folded his hands. \"A friend of mine. Do you know what\ndiablerie is?\"</p>\n<p>Olly knew. It was when one of them drank another one dry, all the way down, and came out stronger.</p>\n<p>\"I dealt with the body,\" Rami said. \"Claude and I have a space in the coroner's office where it is\nhidden, for now. We have been looking for anything that points to who did it. Anything you find would\nbe very valuable to me. This is the kind of thing that brings the hammer down on all of us.\"</p>\n<p>\"You want me on a murder mystery?\" Olly said. \"I'm not an investigator. I bring things places. I scope\nout abandoned buildings. You put drugs in my body.\"</p>\n<p>\"I will be blunt with you.\" Rami gestured at the lab around them, the apparatus bubbling, the careful\npressures. \"If I could trust you with this equipment, I would leave you here to watch it and go myself.\nThere is no one I trust with it. Weeks of work, months. If it boils wrong, if the pressure goes wrong, I\nstart again from nothing, and I do not have the ingredients to start again. So I am here, and you are\nwho I have. You are not my first choice. You are not my second.\" He looked at Olly. \"But it is my friend.\nI do not want to do nothing. And if I wait until I am free, the trail goes cold.\"</p>\n<p>Olly thought about it.</p>\n<p>\"Well,\" he said. \"Naturally I'm forever in your debt for sinking your fangs into me.\"</p>\n<p>He meant it. There wasn't any irony in it at all.</p>\n<p>\"So yes. I'll go.\"</p>", "part": "Two", "played": "2025-11-04", "slug": "03-stupid-or-corrupt", "title": "Stupid or Corrupt", "words": 4522}, {"html": "<h2>The Drawer</h2>\n<p>The morgue was the kind of place Olly was good at.</p>\n<p>Not the medicine. He didn't know the first thing about the medicine. But a building is a building,\nand every building has the way it wants you to walk through it and the way you actually can. He could\nhave put on a hi-vis vest and carried a clipboard. That works almost anywhere. Instead he went the\nlong way, peering round corners, and twice he came round one and found a whole hallway he hadn't\nknown was there, that anyone could have come down while he stood in it. He filed those away for the\ntrip out.</p>\n<p>Claude had a room with a separate key. Rami had told him which drawer.</p>\n<p>The body inside was naked. Not rotted. Not old. Dried, somehow, like something that had been emptied\nand left in a warm place: a husk of what it had been, without the years that ought to go with that.\nThere were tattoos on it. There were scars that weren't accidents, patterns cut on purpose.</p>\n<p>Olly didn't know what a coroner would see. He knew what <em>he</em> saw, because he'd lived it for a year and\na half. The marks where things had gone in. The places the skin had been worked. This one had been a\nfurnace. An athanor, like him: something cooking inside it, distilling, and from the way it had dried\nout, whatever it had been cooking wasn't in there anymore.</p>\n<p>Someone had drained it. All of it.</p>\n<p>He looked at the tattoos a long time. He knew some things about the ink, the way anyone in the scene\ndid. Thin-bloods caught on the Camarilla's ground without somebody to answer for them tended not to\nleave it. There were only two ways around that. Walk in behind someone with enough weight to tell\nthe killers to fuck off, or be <em>registered</em>: branded, permanently, so the court knew who you were and\nwho you belonged to, and extended you the courtesy of not destroying you just for existing.</p>\n<p>Some of the markings on the body reminded him of that. But there was no brand. These were something\nelse, and he didn't know what.</p>\n<p>He knew who might. Darya did ink. Blood-alchemy ink, the only kind that holds in dead skin, and for the\npeople who didn't want to commit to the brand and everything that came with it, she did something that\npassed for one. And she'd be at the COAG this week. The COAG was a pop-up, the Kindred equivalent of\none of Olly's own parties: a location that moved, and if you knew, you knew. This week it was in Lower\nQueen station, the one under Queen Street that had been sealed up since the sixties. Right in the\nmiddle of Camarilla ground.</p>\n<p>He took pictures of every mark. He always had his camera.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>\"Besides you,\" Rami said, looking at the photographs, \"I know of no other in this entire city. And\nthat one was not one I knew of.\"</p>\n<p>\"If this guy had a target on his back,\" Olly said, \"I wonder if I've had one this whole time. I don't\nknow how many people know about our arrangement.\"</p>\n<p>\"Let me put it this way. The kind of people who know would far rather have you working. You are much\nmore valuable in your function than as a corpse.\"</p>\n<p>\"I see,\" Olly said.</p>\n<p>He did not see. Something itched at the back of his brain and didn't stop. Rami had never, in a year\nand a half, cared how Olly <em>felt</em> about anything. It wasn't like him to start now.</p>\n<h2>The Bail Bonds Office</h2>\n<p>Ethan's office was not in a good part of town, which was the point.</p>\n<p>A regular office from the front. Behind the glass door, a second door of bars. Roll-down steel shutters\non the windows. A holding cell with a bunk and a toilet and nothing else, and a heavy door on a room full\nof body armour and weapons that were only mostly not lethal. Cameras on everything, including the cell.\nBreak in, and you'd get there eventually, but everyone in the neighbourhood would hear you.</p>\n<p>Ralph texted his roommate that he wasn't coming home. <em>Got a place to crash, no windows or anything.</em></p>\n<p><em>Lucky you</em>, the roommate wrote back.</p>\n<p>The cell door was unlocked. Ralph could have walked out any time. He'd also have been on camera the whole\nway, and he knew that, and so did Ethan.</p>\n<p>He woke the next night hungry and did what he always did when he woke up hungry and couldn't eat yet:\nsit-ups. Push-ups. His whole routine, like the guy in <em>American Psycho</em>. None of it did anything for a\nbody like his anymore. The routine was the point.</p>\n<p>Ethan found him on the floor of the cell, mid-set.</p>\n<p>\"Morning,\" Ethan said. \"Evening. Whatever.\"</p>\n<p>Rose had the bone now. Somewhere safe, somewhere neither of them had been told. Ralph trusted that as far\nas he had to.</p>\n<p>\"That bone,\" Ethan said. \"It's going to paint a target on your back. It already has, apparently. You need\nfriends. And you need friends with weight. I'm just being blunt with you.\"</p>\n<p>\"I know it's important,\" Ralph said. \"I know the kind of people who want it don't fuck around.\"</p>\n<p>\"What kind of people do you think those are?\"</p>\n<p>\"Old ones. Strong ones.\"</p>\n<p>Ethan didn't know much more than that himself. The Baron of Kensington, Evelyn, was good to thin-bloods,\nbetter than most. She was also strange, and deep into things Ethan kept a careful distance from: ghosts,\nspells, the kind of thing he'd rather not have explained to him. Rose had made him, and Rose was showing\nhim the ropes, and he was, by his own reckoning, not the brightest crayon in the box. Trying to drink from\na fire hose wasn't going to work. So he took the bits and pieces she gave him and didn't push.</p>\n<p>Rose never texted. She used envelopes, always the same kind, always to the same address, always the same\nfew words, just enough to take the next step. The one waiting tonight said she had <em>multiple interested\nparties</em>. They should come to the COAG and meet them.</p>\n<p>\"Looks like there's a lot of interest,\" Ethan said.</p>\n<p>\"I wasn't looking to sell it,\" Ralph said. \"But for the right price.\" He thought about it. \"It'd be a chance\nto find out more about it. If they're interested, they know more than I do. No offense.\"</p>\n<p>\"She knows more than I do. She's old.\" Ethan shrugged. \"I can't speak for her.\"</p>\n<p>\"Why did she want you to bring me here? I figured it was some kind of trap. Apparently not, since I'm not\nlocked up.\"</p>\n<p>\"Safe location. We don't know where you keep it; you do. So nothing happens to you. The doors lock from\noutside, but you can open them. I wouldn't go strolling around this neighbourhood at night. But I'm not\ngoing to stop you.\"</p>\n<p>Ralph had a night to himself before the COAG. He meant to spend some of it getting the bone.</p>\n<p>\"You want me to come?\" Ethan said.</p>\n<p>\"No,\" Ralph said. \"Obviously not. I'm trusting you a lot. Just not to the level of where I keep my stuff when\nI don't want it found. No offense.\"</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Before he went out, Ethan sat down and did what Rose had taught him.</p>\n<p>He still had the fire in him, two deep burns that hadn't closed. The first time he'd done this she had\ncoached him through it, sitting there concentrating, putting his whole weight behind one idea, and it had\nwrung him out like a half-marathon, left him ravenous. This time he just sat and applied what willpower he\nhad to the thought of not hurting anymore, and the burns closed, and it cost him less than he'd expected.\nExperience, maybe.</p>\n<p>He was still hungry. He was usually hungry.</p>\n<h2>Three Suppers</h2>\n<p>Most of Ethan's clients couldn't make bail. That was sort of the business.</p>\n<p>The calls came after the bars closed, two o'clock onward, when people got caught up in whatever they got\ncaught up in. A night guy took them and texted Ethan when there was <em>one for you</em>. It was understood around\nthe office, the way things are understood without anybody saying them, that if a sex worker got picked up and\ncouldn't pay the fee, Ethan would sometimes arrange an alternative. The staff assumed what they assumed. They\nwere wrong, and it suited him that they were wrong.</p>\n<p>And a bail jumper who'd skipped his court date was a lot easier to bring in a couple of pints short.</p>\n<p>The call that night came in around three. Ethan took it himself. He didn't take too much. He never did.</p>\n<p><em>I had the meal</em>, he thought, on the way back. <em>I just didn't have dessert.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<p>Ralph ate in his own neighbourhood, where he knew the houses.</p>\n<p>He had standards. He wanted clean blood, no drugs, no diseases, which meant cars with <em>26.2</em> stickers on\nthe bumper, or the respectable old couple with the nice garden. He went out in his jogging clothes, made\nhimself quiet, let himself in, took a small drink from someone asleep, and left. He fed often so he never\nhad to take much.</p>\n<p>This house he'd visited before. The last time, he'd found their wifi unsecured and downloaded the app that\nran the doorbell camera and the smart lock, and the house had simply let him have it. Tonight he turned off\nthe front camera from his phone, walked up the path, and the door opened for him.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Olly was on edge, and he knew why: the husk in the drawer. He wanted something that felt like judgement.</p>\n<p>He texted Dmitri first, and waited, and Dmitri didn't answer. So he texted Trieste, and made it sound like\nhe didn't care either way. He never liked needing something from her.</p>\n<p>She picked the place: an old church in a worn-down part of town, a little on the nose. When he came in she\nwas standing up behind the altar, in the dark, holding a knife.</p>\n<p>\"Shall I cut this one loose?\"</p>\n<p>\"Play ball,\" said Olly.</p>\n<p>Trieste cut two cords and a woman got up off the floor. Forties. Suit jacket and slacks, like she'd come\nfrom an office. Gagged. She moved away from Trieste fast and saw Olly and moved sideways.</p>\n<p>\"Where'd you find this one?\" Olly said.</p>\n<p>\"You know Ghislaine Maxwell?\" Trieste put the knife away. \"This one was a secretary for one of the Catholic\nchurches. Functions similarly. Thought you might enjoy it.\" She headed for the door. \"We're doing another\nsunrise in a few nights, if you want to come.\"</p>\n<p>\"You know I'll be there.\"</p>\n<p>\"Bring your friends.\"</p>\n<p>Olly went up into the rafters. The woman ran one way, then another, and he tracked her from above and dropped\ndown in front of her, and she stopped dead and didn't scream and didn't faint. She just froze.</p>\n<p>That was what he liked. Like the kind of lizard that won't eat anything that isn't moving: you let the\ncrickets out and close the doors and let it hunt. Olly wanted his prey running.</p>\n<p>He didn't kill her. He left her on the steps of a police station, which seemed to him to say something. He\nwasn't sure what, exactly. But he needed it to mean something, or he couldn't do it at all.</p>\n<h2>Jason Cho</h2>\n<p>Ralph was getting ready for the day, back in his own basement, when the doorbell rang.</p>\n<p>He checked the camera. A man in a good suit on the front step. The same man his roommate had described.</p>\n<p>Ralph went up with a baseball bat and held it out of sight behind the door frame.</p>\n<p>\"Oh, violence won't be necessary,\" the man said, pleasantly, before Ralph had said a word. He had a tablet.\n\"The name's Jason Cho. I heard you may have something of interest. I'd be happy to buy it from you.\"</p>\n<p>\"What object would that be?\"</p>\n<p>\"I think you know. Unless you have more than one.\"</p>\n<p>\"What are you offering?\"</p>\n<p>\"We can start at five million.\"</p>\n<p>Ralph didn't say anything for a moment.</p>\n<p>\"I see I've got your attention,\" said Jason Cho.</p>\n<p>\"Yes. Yes, you have. I knew it was valuable. I didn't know it was <em>that</em> valuable.\"</p>\n<p>\"It's all a matter of valuable to whom.\" Cho was open to suggestions about how to move that much. He'd done\nblockchain. He'd done gold. He could do the transaction here and now, if Ralph had the item.</p>\n<p>\"It's in a safety deposit box,\" Ralph said. \"At a bank. Because I knew it was valuable. Do you have an office?\nWe could meet tomorrow.\"</p>\n<p>No office. Instead Cho held up the tablet with a QR code on it: scan it, request five million, sign the terms,\nand he'd negotiate the transfer. Then he held out his hand.</p>\n<p>Ralph shook it and paid attention. Warm. Breathing. Blinking. And under all of that, no pulse. Cho was exactly\nas dead as Ralph was, and wearing it just as well.</p>\n<p>Ralph took a photo of the QR code. He did not scan it. You don't scan a stranger's QR code. Nothing could\npossibly go wrong with that.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>The next evening his roommate was at the kitchen table, scrolling on his phone, and there was a business card\non the table beside him. <em>Jason Cho — Collector.</em> No number. No address. Nothing.</p>\n<p>\"Guy came by for you last night. Corporate type. Reporter or agent or something.\"</p>\n<p>Ralph stuck the card on the fridge. \"I found an antique. I've been trying to auction it off. It's worth more\nthan I thought. I'm meeting buyers tomorrow.\"</p>\n<p>\"Like a book?\"</p>\n<p>\"It's an old bone. From Greece or something.\"</p>\n<p>\"What stops anyone from just carving an old bone?\"</p>\n<p>\"It has to be a certain age. Experts can tell. Same reason you can't just paint a Picasso.\"</p>\n<p>\"There are forgers.\"</p>\n<p>\"There are. These people are sending lawyers around. They're serious.\"</p>\n<p>\"Well,\" said the roommate, \"don't mix me up with the lawyers,\" and went to bed.</p>\n<h2>Lower Queen</h2>\n<p>Ralph called the office when he woke. Ethan wasn't in yet. They offered to have him call back; Ralph said\ndon't bother, it's time-sensitive, he'll want to talk to me. Ethan called back the moment he woke.</p>\n<p>\"Long time no talk. I'm coming to the office. Something's come up. It might change plans.\"</p>\n<p>He came in with a hard-shell Pelican case. He'd put a couple of road flares in it from the garage. He had the\nbone under his jacket.</p>\n<p>\"I had a surprise visitor right before I went down for the day. You know a guy named, and I use the word <em>guy</em>\nloosely, Jason Cho?\"</p>\n<p>Ethan didn't. The name didn't ring any bell at all. The number did. Ethan had once thought he was doing\namazing because he'd cleared a hundred grand in a year.</p>\n<p>\"Look,\" he said. \"If you want to sell it, sell it. But that was his <em>first</em> offer. He came looking for you. You\nmight come out further ahead.\"</p>\n<p>\"With that kind of money,\" Ralph said, \"I always worry the price difference between paying me and killing me\ngets very, very small.\"</p>\n<p>He still wanted someone watching his back, whoever he sold to. He'd spread the wealth for it. Cho wasn't alive,\nhe was pretty sure.</p>\n<p>Ethan didn't know what Rose wanted, exactly. He knew she was a power player, or at least, from where he stood,\nthat she said things and then things got done, or people got hurt. As long as he took care of his boss, his boss\nwould take care of him. And what he'd been told was: keep the guy alive, or unalive, and keep the bone safe.\nAt least until the meeting.</p>\n<p>They took the company car, an intentionally forgettable Honda Civic, a bit beat-up, the kind of car that blends\nin better in a bad neighbourhood than a good one, and drove down toward Queen Street.</p>\n<p>Two thin-bloods, one of them carrying a bone worth five million dollars, driving into the Prince's ground.</p>\n<p>Nothing happened. That was almost worse. There was one moment, a car that might have been paying attention to\nthem and then wasn't, like passing a state trooper doing ten over, and nothing came of it. But by the time they\ncame down into the old station, Ethan's blood was up, and Ralph could feel it in himself too.</p>\n<p>Rose was waiting, and she was irritable.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>The COAG was not Elysium. Ethan had heard of Elysium, the Camarilla's neutral ground, with rules. This was lower\nclass than that and lower key, run on a gentleman's agreement. It looked, more than anything, like a small flea\nmarket or a new-age fair that had been set up in a tunnel: some vendors with semi-private booths for doing real\nbusiness, and out in the open, crystals and love potions and occult bric-a-brac for the mortals who came for the\nedge of it all. Some of those mortals were ghouls. Everybody was there for the strange. There was a lot of talking.\nThere were a lot of stories.</p>\n<p>A hundred Kindred in Toronto, people said, at least, and probably too many. And more ghouls, and more hangers-on\nthan that.</p>\n<p>Ralph didn't look at what was for sale. He looked at who was buying.</p>\n<p>Most of them were marks: people with money who bought whatever had the right look and a good pitch. What interested\nhim were the outliers, and they were outliers in every direction. A man who looked homeless holding his own, as an\nequal, in a conversation with a banker in a very good suit. Someone in scrubs trousers with a jacket thrown over them,\nstraight from a shift. Two kids who couldn't have been more than fourteen or fifteen, running their own stall.</p>\n<p>And across the room, a sharp suit and pomaded hair, different suit than last night. Jason Cho. Moving through the\ncrowd like he was looking for somebody.</p>\n<h2>The Mark</h2>\n<p>Rose was blunt with Ralph.</p>\n<p>\"Excuse me,\" she said, \"but you seem exceptionally ignorant. Do you know what a Methuselah is?\"</p>\n<p>He didn't.</p>\n<p>What she told him then, he only half followed: something about the Kindred who were much older, much stronger, who\nhad died a long time ago. What her sources said the bone had to do with that. If they were right, she said, it was worth\nfar more than money. She wanted to make some deals. She had buyers here who would pay dearly, but they'd need to know\nit was real, and have the chance to possess it, if it came to that.</p>\n<p>Ralph showed her Jason Cho's card.</p>\n<p>Her face did something, a frown, quickly erased. She handed the card back.</p>\n<p>\"He approached you?\"</p>\n<p>\"Yes. And made a very sizable offer.\"</p>\n<p>\"I believe it.\" She looked at him. \"What do you need, out of this deal?\"</p>\n<p>\"Two things, pulling different ways,\" Ralph said. \"I need to learn about what I am, and what I'm being dragged into.\nNot <em>dragged</em>. I'm doing this willingly. I'm looking forward to immortality. I need to know all of it. I need people I\ncan trust to watch my back, and whose backs I can watch, so I don't get squished by somebody older than me. And I need\nmoney. I have expensive tastes. I like money.\"</p>\n<p>Rose nodded, as if that was about what she'd expected.</p>\n<p>To meet the buyers here, she said, the two of them needed a mark. There were people in this room who would not deal\nwith two thin-bloods walking around loose. The only way she could bring them in was if they carried something that said\nthey were hers, answerable to her, under her seat.</p>\n<p>She showed them. A few sweeping lines, nothing fancy.</p>\n<p>Once he'd seen it, Ralph started seeing it on other people around the room.</p>\n<p>\"A temporary tattoo,\" said Ethan. \"Okay. Weird. Sure.\"</p>\n<h2>Lil Sis</h2>\n<p>Olly was working the room in code.</p>\n<p>He wasn't sure he needed to. Everybody here was a vampire; it wasn't like the cops were listening. But you buy drugs by\ntalking around them, and old habits were old habits. He drifted down the little side passages off the main tunnel and back\nout, talking to people who looked like they might be into it, and down one of the nooks he found Todd.</p>\n<p>\"You okay, man? What happened after I saw you? Did you guys get out all right?\"</p>\n<p>\"I'm just glad I got out with my head,\" Todd said. \"I don't know what the fuck that was about. We're a complete bust. I'm\ntrying to go independent.\" He patted a suitcase. \"I've got a stash, at least.\"</p>\n<p>The suitcase was full of little bottles, and every one of them was a mood. One would get you drunk, and ran phlegmatic.\nOne was supposed to give you sleep with good dreams. Six or eight of them, all needing their particular kind of blood and\ntime to brew, and a furnace. Todd wanted cash, or opportunities with Rami.</p>\n<p>\"Rami doesn't party,\" Olly said. \"He wants nothing to do with this stuff. I want to be where <em>you</em> are. On your level. I\ncan pay. Or I can do favours for you. We don't need to bring Rami into it.\"</p>\n<p>Todd thought about it. \"Does he have downtime? If we needed lab equipment?\"</p>\n<p>\"Lab equipment,\" said Olly, \"is not a problem for me.\"</p>\n<p>Todd wrote down an address; they'd meet up after, and talk. Then he let Olly pick. Olly picked the melancholic one,\nbecause it wasn't his usual.</p>\n<p>\"I call that one the Strength of Sadness,\" Todd said. \"It's good for when you need your defences up. Emotionally and\notherwise.\"</p>\n<p>He'd spotted where Darya was working. He was heading back out into the main tunnel when he stopped short.</p>\n<p>Rose. The guy who'd caught fire. And a third one he didn't know. Walking together across the open floor.</p>\n<p>The last time he'd seen those people he'd nearly been burned alive. He found somewhere else to be, quietly, where they\nwouldn't notice him.</p>\n<p>Somebody clapped him on the back.</p>\n<p>\"Hey! How you doing?\" It was Lil Sis, from Trieste's crew. \"Haven't seen you since the rooftop. How's your friend?\"</p>\n<p>\"Oh, we're good. I wish I was out on the roof more often. Have you guys been out since?\"</p>\n<p>\"Not since Jonah got crispy.\" She said it the way you'd say it rained. \"That was sad, how that happened. But I guess that's\njust how it goes. It's going to be all our turns sometime.\"</p>\n<p>\"Maybe,\" said Olly. \"But if you didn't sit out on the roof waiting for the sun, it wouldn't have to be. I'd rather\nchoose my time a little. Not all the way. A little.\"</p>\n<p>\"Yeah,\" said Lil Sis. \"I see what you're saying.\"</p>\n<p>\"It was great being out there with you guys, though. What are you here for?\"</p>\n<p>\"Malik had some stuff to offload. We found a score. Couple of old books.\"</p>\n<p>The way she told it, and the way she didn't, Olly understood where they'd come from: a Ravnos stash, somebody's cache of\ntreasures, cleaned out. Across the room, Ethan was listening too, and came to the same conclusion.</p>\n<p>Somewhere around then the two of them clocked each other. Neither of them said so.</p>\n<h2>The Frontrunner</h2>\n<p>Rose split off to her meetings: some for the bone, some her own. She'd be behind a curtain in one of the booths, close\nenough to shout for.</p>\n<p>Ethan and Ralph got in line for Darya.</p>\n<p>\"So you're a legit skip tracer,\" Ralph said. \"Bounty hunter. Bail bondsman. I assume you can handle yourself if things get\ncritical?\"</p>\n<p>\"Nothing too crazy,\" said Ethan. \"But yeah. With humans. Maybe a couple more than one at a time. That's why my\neyes don't stay still.\"</p>\n<p>Jason Cho came through the crowd in a straight line toward them.</p>\n<p>\"Hey,\" Ethan said, low. \"You know that guy?\"</p>\n<p>\"That's the guy.\"</p>\n<p>Cho put his hand out to Ethan, warmly. \"Jason Cho. And you are?\"</p>\n<p>\"Ethan. Pleased to meet you.\"</p>\n<p>\"Likewise. Are you an associate of our friend here?\"</p>\n<p>\"Yeah. We're partners.\"</p>\n<p>\"Well.\" Cho turned to Ralph. \"I must say I'm sad not to have heard from you yet. Is there anything I can answer?\"</p>\n<p>\"Not yet. You're still the frontrunner.\"</p>\n<p>\"Happy to hear it. If there's anything I can do to keep things smooth, just let me know. If you have a competing offer,\nI'm happy to discuss. In all likelihood I'll check in with you before I leave.\"</p>\n<p>Ethan went in first, with the case on his lap and Rose's voice somewhere on the other side of a curtain. He kept his ears\nopen for Ralph.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Olly went looking for Darya and found her assistant instead.</p>\n<p>\"I don't need ink today,\" he said. \"But I had some questions, and I thought she'd be the person to answer them. Is there\nany chance she's got a minute? I know she's busy. Everybody's here for her.\"</p>\n<p>\"I'll see if we can fit you in. It's a working night; she's filling all her blocks. But if somebody finishes early, hang\naround. You might catch her for a few minutes between clients.\"</p>\n<p>\"That's all I need. I'll hang out.\"</p>\n<p>So he was loitering outside Darya's booth when the man with the bone case, the one Ethan had been walking with, came and\nstood next to him.</p>\n<p>\"You have a briefcase?\" Olly said. The guy didn't; he'd handed it off.</p>\n<p>\"Ethan's got it. Ethan's the one getting tattooed.\"</p>\n<p>\"Oh. Right.\" Olly looked at the curtain. \"He looks better than last time. A little less crispy. Those guys you're with,\nfriends of yours?\"</p>\n<p>\"Maybe. New friends.\" The man put out a hand. \"Ralph.\"</p>\n<p>\"Olly. It's always good to make new friends. Also good to know who you're running with.\" He dropped his voice a little.\n\"I ran into those guys at an operation about a week ago. Kind of a domineering vibe. Things got violent. I'm not saying it\nwas on them. But I wonder how you got wrapped up in what they're doing.\"</p>\n<p>\"We have mutual business interests.\"</p>\n<p>\"Well. I hope you're on their side of that business.\"</p>\n<p>\"What's your business?\"</p>\n<p>\"Me? I throw parties. I move things around. I know people, I know places.\" Olly waved at the tunnel. \"We had a party right\nhere, a few months back. Huge. I think I saw you?\"</p>\n<p>\"No. I only came back from California a couple of months ago.\"</p>\n<p>\"Oh. You've just got one of those faces.\"</p>\n<p>\"One of those astoundingly beautiful faces.\"</p>\n<p>\"Words out of my mouth,\" said Olly.</p>\n<p>Ethan came out. Ralph went in. Ethan took up a post outside the curtain like a club bouncer, watching everybody who came\nnear, listening for any noise out of Ralph at all.</p>\n<p>The guy with the phone, leaning the other way and scrolling, didn't look like a threat. He didn't look like anything. But\nabout five minutes in, Ethan noticed he wouldn't make eye contact, and kept not making it, and slowly Ethan remembered why.</p>\n<p>He walked over. \"I know you, don't I?\"</p>\n<p>\"A lot of people know me around here. You do look familiar.\"</p>\n<p>\"No. I remember you. You were in that basement.\"</p>\n<p>\"Oh my God,\" said Olly, as if it had only just come back to him. \"That guy burned you.\"</p>\n<p>\"Yeah,\" said Ethan. \"That fucking hurt.\"</p>\n<p>\"You okay? You're in pretty good shape for what happened.\"</p>\n<p>\"Mostly okay. Yeah.\"</p>\n<p>\"Glad to hear it. That was not good.\"</p>\n<p>\"Was that a friend of yours? The flamethrower guy?\"</p>\n<p>\"No. I'm a peaceful guy, really. I wouldn't get wrapped up in something like that.\"</p>\n<p>Everything about the truth, Olly thought, was the wrong thing to lead with. That he'd been stoned out of his mind the\nwhole time, for one. That those were his people, sort of, whose stuff these guys had been stealing. So he gave Ethan the\nversion where he was just a delivery guy waiting for a signature, wrong place, wrong time, and he gave it well.</p>\n<p>\"Sometimes I run packages,\" he said. \"Pay the vampire bills. You know how it is. I just had a job. I was about to get out\nof there, and then things went down. Sorry I couldn't have done more. Honestly.\"</p>\n<p>Ethan heard all of it. He also heard what was under it: a nervous, paranoid <em>please don't poke at this</em>. And he\nremembered a gun, down at this guy's side.</p>\n<p><em>Then why'd you have a gun in your hand?</em> He didn't say it.</p>\n<p>\"All right,\" he said instead. \"So you were just there. Wrong place, wrong time. That's what you're saying.\"</p>\n<h2>The Sheriff</h2>\n<p>Something at the edge of Ethan's eye made him look up.</p>\n<p>A man, very tall, six foot plenty, in a suit that was for working in, not for looking at. He was standing over two\nsmaller figures, and it was about to turn into something. The two smaller figures were the ones Olly had been talking to.</p>\n<p>Ethan knew who the tall man was. He'd been told who to tip his hat to in this city, and who not to cross at all. That\nwas Elijah Kane. The Sheriff.</p>\n<p>\"Looks like your friends are in some shit,\" Ethan said.</p>\n<p>\"Oh, shit.\" Olly looked. \"I'm sure it's a misunderstanding.\"</p>\n<p>\"Misunderstandings follow you around. Is that how that works?\"</p>\n<p>\"I'm so misunderstood.\"</p>\n<p>Olly edged closer, trying to hear. He couldn't make out what the Sheriff's problem was. He could only hear how hot it\nwas running.</p>\n<p>Then he did the stupidest thing he'd done all year, which was a competitive category. He walked straight into it.</p>\n<p>\"What seems to be the problem here?\" he said. \"Officer?\"</p>\n<p>The Sheriff turned and looked down at him. \"These piece-of-shit thin-bloods yours?\"</p>\n<p>\"They're friends of mine. Good folks. Seems like there must have been some kind of confusion, if you've got a problem\nwith them.\"</p>\n<p>\"And your name is?\"</p>\n<p>\"Todd,\" said Olly.</p>\n<p>\"Todd. And your sire?\"</p>\n<p>This was the part where honesty would have been a mistake. You didn't just make one of them in this city; you asked the\nPrince first, and Olly was nearly sure Rami never had. So he named a different Banu Haqim, one he'd met once and hadn't\nliked, and put everything he had into saying it like it was true.</p>\n<p>The Sheriff looked at him for a long moment. His face screwed up, like a man tasting something he couldn't place.</p>\n<p>\"Take care of it myself,\" he said.</p>\n<p>He took out zip ties. He bound Lil Sis's hands behind her, then Malik's. Then he picked them up, one in each hand, like\nluggage.</p>\n<p>\"I'll bring you to Tash myself.\"</p>\n<p>He walked away with them through the crowd. Olly didn't know who Tash was. He did notice, standing there with his hands\nfree, that he was not the third thing being carried.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Ralph came out of Darya's booth with fresh lines on his skin in time to see the end of it.</p>\n<p>\"Is that something that's supposed to happen?\"</p>\n<p>\"That was the Sheriff,\" Ethan said. \"Far as I understand it, as long as he's doing what the Prince wants, he can do\nwhatever he wants.\"</p>\n<p>\"That Prince word again. I assume <em>Sheriff</em> is another one of those capitalized words.\"</p>\n<p>\"There's a few of them,\" said Ethan. \"That's a big one.\"</p>\n<p>Rose came back. She was pleased they both had their marks. She smiled.</p>\n<p>\"The real one,\" she said, \"is going to be Dominik Levesque. Tremere. Very influential, and a serious scholar of the occult.\nHe's the one I'm hoping works out. The meeting I just came out of is our contingency. And leverage. We have options. This\nis great.\"</p>\n<p>Ralph asked what Jason Cho was likely to do, if he was disappointed.</p>\n<p>Rose thought about it. \"He's not buying for himself. Somebody sent him. The question is who. Until we know that, I can't\ntell you how much trouble he'll be.\"</p>\n<h2>Tash</h2>\n<p>Olly called Trieste on the move, following the Sheriff up and out of the station at a distance.</p>\n<p>\"I just saw Lil Sis and Malik get into some shit with the Sheriff. He took them. I know they just robbed what I think was\na Ravnos stash somewhere, so who knows what they're into. I'm following them now. He said he was taking them to Tash.\"</p>\n<p>Trieste went quiet. When she spoke again it was low and measured, in a voice he had never heard out of her.</p>\n<p>\"Tash is the one who does the branding,\" she said. \"They're not going to be branded. They're going to their final death.\"</p>\n<p>\"Okay.\"</p>\n<p>\"I'll get Zuzu. We're going to have to break them out. We're going to have to go in hard. I've got some weaponry I can\nbring. Where do you want to meet?\"</p>\n<p>\"Tell us where. We'll go in hot.\" He could see the Sheriff ahead of him, a head taller than everyone on the street, a\ncaptive in each hand. \"Look, he's huge. This Sheriff. I don't know if you've seen him. He's gigantic. Are you sure the three\nof us can pull this off?\"</p>\n<p>\"All we have to do is get to Lil Sis and Malik. Get them loose so they can act on their own, and then we split up and\nscatter.\"</p>\n<p>\"Maybe we call in a favour. I could check with Rami. He might know somebody.\"</p>\n<p>\"We'll be there. You're at the COAG? We can be there in twenty-five minutes. Can you follow them? Keep us posted where\nthey're going?\"</p>\n<p>\"Yeah. If you can get here sooner, the move might be to hit them before they get wherever they're going. Before the\nsecond location.\"</p>\n<p>He put his phone away and kept walking, far enough back, in the Sheriff's shadow.</p>\n<p>Nobody noticed him.</p>", "part": "Two", "played": "2025-11-18", "slug": "04-the-coag", "title": "The COAG", "words": 5676}, {"html": "<h2>Lab Rats</h2>\n<p>Olly called Rami on the move, half a block behind the Sheriff.</p>\n<p>Rami picked up, which was its own kind of luck. \"Say your piece.\"</p>\n<p>Olly said it badly. The Sheriff. Friends of his. Something about a Ravnos stash, and trouble for it, and\nsomebody called Tash at the other end.</p>\n<p>\"You,\" said Rami, \"are a prime fucking piece of lab equipment. Do not mess around with the Sheriff. What are you\ndoing?\"</p>\n<p>\"Some friends are getting dragged toward execution. Do you know the executioner in this area?\"</p>\n<p>There was a pause while Rami worked out what he'd actually been told. The Sheriff hadn't killed them where they\nstood. He wasn't making an example of them in the street. He was taking them somewhere.</p>\n<p>\"Tash,\" Rami said, when Olly gave him the name. \"That would be Tatiana Mirek. She is part of a chantry. Over by\nWellesley. The Queen's Park area.\" Another pause. \"That's probably worse. Your friends are going to be lab\nrats.\"</p>\n<p>He asked what Olly owed them. Olly said they were close with Trieste.</p>\n<p>\"So, don't care. They made a mistake. You should not make the same mistake.\"</p>\n<p>He left a silence, the kind he used to give you one chance to be stupid out loud. Olly didn't take it. Rami was\nabout to hang up. Then, instead:</p>\n<p>\"Do you have any concoctions on you?\"</p>\n<p>\"I just got one.\"</p>\n<p>\"What's the flavour?\"</p>\n<p>\"Melancholic. He calls it the Strength of Sadness.\"</p>\n<p>\"Yes,\" Rami said. \"Yes. That would be—\" And he stopped himself, and Olly could hear him stop. \"No. Just forget\nthey existed. That's not your problem.\"</p>\n<p>The line went dead.</p>\n<p>Olly knew why Rami cared, the little that he did. There had been one before him. Another thin-blood, another\nfurnace, and that one had been destroyed, and Rami had had to go out and find a replacement. The replacement was\nOlly. Rami was careful with his tools.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>He kept following.</p>\n<p>Queen's Park he knew. He hadn't been up there much since he died, but he knew the way, and he knew the quick way,\nand he knew he could get there before the Sheriff did. What he didn't know was what to do once he was there. Wait for\nTrieste, and let them get behind whatever door they were going behind, where a rescue would be ten times harder. Or\ntry something now, alone.</p>\n<p>The Sheriff didn't hurry. He didn't have to. He had Lil Sis and Malik by the arms, lifted just enough that their feet\nbarely touched the pavement, the way a grown man walks a little kid who doesn't want to go. They were nominally\nwalking. They weren't going anywhere he didn't want.</p>\n<p>He was huge. Shooting him from a rooftop seemed like a bet. Olly looked at the cars on the street instead.</p>\n<p>At that time of night the problem wasn't finding one. It was starting one. But Olly had some expertise there, and\nsome time. He picked an SUV. He got it going. He took the gun out of his bag and put it on the seat beside him, in\ncase whatever came after the collision needed finishing.</p>\n<p>There was the question of the other two. The Sheriff had one of them in each hand. Hitting the middle and missing the\nedges was not a thing anyone could do with a car. Hitting two, and choosing which one took less of it: that was\npossible. It was a trolley problem, and Olly had about a block to solve it.</p>\n<p>Lil Sis, he decided. She looked less squishy.</p>\n<p>He let them pass. Then he hit the high beams, for the theatre of it, so the big man would have a second to wonder what\nwas happening, and put his foot down.</p>\n<h2>The Chantry</h2>\n<p>On the other side of the same stretch of night, Rose was driving Ethan and Ralph to meet a Tremere.</p>\n<p>Ethan had heard of the Tremere. Everybody talked a little smack about the Tremere, even when they did it with fear and\ntrembling. A magical order, the story went, older than it had any right to be; not even vampires to begin with,\npeople said, until they'd found some sorcerous way to graft themselves into the blood. Anything occult came back round\nto them sooner or later. They kept houses for it, chantries, and some of those were buildings and some were more like a\nuniversity department: a clade of researchers sharing books and methods. They also tended to sit close to power. Which\nwas why Ethan had never met one. He'd spent his nights so far staying out of the Camarilla's part of the city.</p>\n<p>Rose parked by the library. Phones and anything else with a battery went into the glove compartment.</p>\n<p>\"Let me do the talking,\" she said. She looked at Ralph. \"You have it on you?\"</p>\n<p>\"Of course.\"</p>\n<p>The door was in the side of one of the old stone buildings: heavy, iron, the kind of door that looks like it was\nretired when the building got modernized and somebody moved the real entrance round the corner. Rose reached up and took\nhold of the big handle, which looked like a plug, and pried it off with both hands. It was a magnet. She set it against\na different part of the door, and something inside clicked softly, and she pulled the door open on a very dim light. She\nput the plug back and waved them in ahead of her.</p>\n<p>It was a wacky door. Ethan kept that to himself. Head on a swivel. Pay attention. Don't put your foot in your\nmouth. Watch Rose for any sign that he was about to fuck something up.</p>\n<p>Up three flights. A gallery overlooking a garden, a real one, growing in the middle of the building. Past it, a short\nstair to what should have been a service level, storage, pipes. Instead there was carpet, beautiful carpet, and paintings,\nand soft light, and music from somewhere. A ghoul in good clothes opened the doors and took their names and offered to\ntake their coats. It was like one of those clubs styled on a century or two ago. There were drinks, a formality; there\nwas something in them that was probably bagged blood, not fresh, but enough to give it a little vim.</p>\n<p>And there were books. Tomes open on wooden stands, incredibly thick. Strange devices of brass set at particular angles,\nthat looked like they might be tracking planets, or something else.</p>\n<p>Ralph slowed down at the books. He had Spanish and English and Russian, and he wanted to know if any of this was in\none of them. Most of it he couldn't even place the alphabet. One he nearly could: letters like Cyrillic, arranged in no\npattern he'd ever seen. The way Farsi wears the Arabic script, it was a script he knew on a language he didn't. He stood\nover it anyway, and moved his lips a little, as if reading a passage, in case one word stuck that he could drop into\nconversation later.</p>\n<p>The man who came to meet them introduced himself as Dominik Levesque. He shook Rose's hand.</p>\n<p>\"Welcome. I do believe you have an offer of sorts. Please, let us see this device.\"</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Ralph had been playing the shell game all night. The Pelican case, the jacket, the case again. He held out his hand to\nEthan for the case now, and opened it, and if anybody in that room noticed that the bone had not been in the case until\nthe moment it came out of the case, nobody's face said so.</p>\n<p>He looked at it again himself, in that light, in that room. It looked like what it had always looked like. A human femur.\nThe strange writing on it.</p>\n<p>He set it down on a cloth on the table, with some ceremony. You can always wash a cloth.</p>\n<p>Dominik bent to it, all the way at the waist, and peered at it from very close. He didn't touch it. He ran his fingers a\nfew millimetres above its surface, all along its length, and talked to himself too quietly to follow.</p>\n<p>Ethan listened to the building. Apart from the four of them there was nobody in the room. The ghoul was down the hall.\nEvery door they'd passed stood open, rooms running into rooms, and he hadn't seen or heard another soul.</p>\n<p>\"Initial assessments,\" Dominik said at last, straightening. \"I think your guess is correct. This seems to be authentic.\nMy estimation would be that this is <em>Tzimisce Haudenosaunee</em>.\"</p>\n<p>He let it sit. \"Obviously we would need more time. But what you are looking at is probably an incredibly potent spell. A\ncurse. Something of that nature. Where did you get this, again?\"</p>\n<p>\"There was quite a tale involved in its acquisition,\" said Ralph. \"Possibly a dangerous tale.\"</p>\n<p>Dominik looked at him properly for the first time. \"I'm sorry. Who are you?\"</p>\n<p>\"My name's Ralph. I'm new.\"</p>\n<p>Dominik looked at Rose, then back. \"You are under her protection?\"</p>\n<p>\"Yes,\" said Ralph.</p>\n<p>\"I see.\" He turned back to her as if Ralph had been answered and filed. \"Well, Rose. As I was saying, this seems quite\nuseful, and valuable. Here we have the means to study it and protect it. My offer: I have a neighbourhood not far from\nhere. Barbara Hall Park, near Church Street. Gloucester. There has not been as much activity there of late. The\nCamarilla could use someone holding down the fort. If you want it as your feeding grounds, I will grant it to you, and my\nprotection, and your passage in Camarilla territory. And if you wish to extend that to those under your protection, I\nsuppose I won't prevent it. Do those terms seem acceptable?\"</p>\n<p>Rose shook on it without looking at either of them.</p>\n<p>\"Do you need anything while we're here?\" she asked them, in the tone of someone who very much hoped not. Transacted.\nDone. Get the fuck out. \"We'll come back in the near future and see what you've learned.\"</p>\n<hr />\n<p>The iron door shut behind them, and they could talk again.</p>\n<p>\"Well,\" said Rose. \"That could have gone a lot worse.\"</p>\n<p>\"I'll take your word for that,\" said Ralph. \"I didn't realize our status was quite that low.\"</p>\n<p>\"Now, you didn't hear this from me, and do not say this to him.\" She kept walking. \"Word has it he tried to sire a\nchilde, and it turned out thin-blooded, and he had it killed. So there was a reason I took the precautions I did.\"</p>\n<p>Ralph thought about his own sire. \"Mine did something similar, I think. Broke my neck.\nLeft me, I assume, to die.\" He considered it. \"They probably should have known that wouldn't do it. So maybe it was meant\nmore as an insult.\"</p>\n<p>\"But the good news.\" Rose almost smiled. \"That's territory we've never had. Ours, with permission. Full writ. I've never\nbeen a huge fan of the Camarilla. But if they're going to give us ground we can feed on without fear of retaliation, I'll\ntake it. And I'm very willing to extend my protection to you two, with it.\"</p>\n<p>They were most of the way back to the car when they heard the engine and the screech.</p>\n<h2>Point Blank</h2>\n<p>Olly got most of what he wanted.</p>\n<p>The Sheriff heard him coming. There's a speed some of them have, when it comes to it, and he could have been gone before\nthe bumper arrived. But that meant letting go of his prisoners, and he would not let go. So he took it. The SUV caught\nhim and Malik together and threw them, into a light post and a garbage can and a mailbox, and Lil Sis was yanked out of\nhis grip the other way with her arm going somewhere arms don't go. Olly stopped short of the curb and the wall. He grabbed\nthe gun and rolled out of the car.</p>\n<p>Lil Sis was on her feet, arm hanging wrong, screaming, \"Malik! Take him! <em>Take</em> him!\"</p>\n<p>The Sheriff had gone over the light post and folded across the mailbox; Malik had hit the mailbox square and bounced off\nthe wall. Malik was still getting up.</p>\n<p>The Sheriff was already up.</p>\n<p>And then he moved, and Olly had never seen one of them move like that. There was no between. The Sheriff was by the\nmailbox and then he was right in front of Olly with a hand on the gun, and what saved Olly was nothing he decided. Pure\nmuscle memory: the step back, the gun swapped to the other hand, the big fingers closing on air.</p>\n<p>He was right there. Olly put the gun against his head and fired.</p>\n<p>It was, he'd think later, like hitting a deer with your car. You get out after, and you finish it, for ethical reasons.</p>\n<p>The Sheriff froze. Shook his head once, like a dog shaking off water. And turned back to Olly, ready to go.</p>\n<p>Olly's scream changed in the middle. It started out as the kind you scream at a man you're shooting in the face and\nfinished as the other kind.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Ethan and Ralph came up on it from the dark side of the street. Rose had taken one look and gone for the car, and on her\nway she'd told Ethan: <em>get close enough to see what's going on. Don't get involved unless you have to.</em> Ethan had put\nhimself between her and the noise anyway, because that was the job, and slid an extendable baton out of his pocket, and\nkept it closed in his fist. He had several at the office. Every once in a while, people need a little encouragement.</p>\n<p>Ralph looked for anything bulletproof: a low wall, a planter, some piece of civic statuary. Cover first. Watching second.</p>\n<p>What they saw was a man get shot in the face and shrug it off.</p>\n<p>It is a little intimidating, watching that.</p>\n<p>Then Lil Sis pulled herself up to her full height and put everything she had into five words.</p>\n<p>\"I want you to stop!\"</p>\n<p>And the Sheriff did.</p>\n<p>He turned toward her, and it was something longer than the bullet: a tug of war with nothing on the rope, the big man\nstraining at some invisible thing and losing, for now. He didn't move.</p>\n<p>Olly backed away. Shooting didn't work. He'd shot the guy in the head, and the guy was fine. He needed space between\nhimself and whatever this was, and to watch, and understand.</p>\n<p>Malik came up behind the Sheriff swigging from a little flask, or something that looked like one. He took a lighter out\nof his pocket. He lit it, and held it in front of his face, and <em>licked</em> it, and fire filled his mouth and ran down his\nthroat like it lived there. He wrapped the Sheriff up from behind in a bear hug and breathed it out, all over him.</p>\n<p>Both of them started to burn.</p>\n<p>All three watching felt the thing in them that wanted to run. Ralph and Ethan held it down. Olly held it down too, and\nthen did something worse, from the point of view of self-preservation. He took his coat off.</p>\n<p><em>It's the right thing to do.</em> They were his friends, sort of. He was responsible for Malik,\nsort of. He beat at the fire with the coat and tried to smother it. The Sheriff was going to be pretty messed up after\nbeing on fire; there were three of them; they could handle the Sheriff. That was the math.</p>\n<p>The fire went down. Malik didn't want it down. He was working against Olly, feeding it, and Olly could feel the heat\ncoming off him from the inside.</p>\n<p>Across the street Ethan and Ralph were observing, and mentally taking notes. It was not their fight.</p>\n<p>Then the Sheriff arched back and shook, and whatever Lil Sis had on him came loose.</p>\n<p>Olly got hold of Malik with both hands. He felt the pull start, the same impossible pull as before, and held on as hard as\nhe'd ever held anything, and Malik's grip on the Sheriff's neck held harder, and what Olly kept was a handful of scorched\nclothing. The rest of Malik was gone.</p>\n<p>A shape went past, between the place Ethan was hiding and the place Ralph was hiding. It stopped at the chantry's iron\ndoor. The Sheriff was there, Malik still on his back, still tussling, and the Sheriff was hammering on the door with his\nfist, <em>wham, wham, wham</em>.</p>\n<p>Then the door was closed and nobody was in front of it.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Rose had the car running in the library lot. She hadn't moved it.</p>\n<p>Lil Sis ran toward the door, and Olly ran after Lil Sis still holding the smoking rags of Malik's clothes, and in doing it\npassed right between Ethan and Ralph without seeing either of them. A window came down as he passed the car. Rose looked\nout at him, oddly.</p>\n<p>\"That's suicide,\" she said to Lil Sis. \"Get in the car.\" The other window came down. \"Ethan. Ralph. Get in the car.\"</p>\n<p>Lil Sis looked from the car to the door and back. She cursed for a while. Then she got in.</p>\n<p>Rose drove, and Ralph took shotgun, because he was important, and Ethan got in the back with the other two because they\nwere exactly the kind of people he wasn't going to turn his back on.</p>\n<p>\"So who the fuck are you,\" said Rose, backing out, \"and why are you attacking the Sheriff in the middle of the street in\nthe middle of the night? What the fuck are you thinking?\"</p>\n<p>\"You can't allow these abductions,\" Olly said. \"Under what justice is this person just taking my friends?\"</p>\n<p>\"That's the Sheriff, and we're in Camarilla territory. Do you want to take it up with the Prince?\" To Lil Sis: \"What did\nyour friends do? What did you do?\"</p>\n<p>\"I didn't do anything. We found that shit from the Ravnos cache and we were at the COAG to see if we could pawn it off.\nBesides. Fuck the Camarilla.\"</p>\n<p>\"That's what I'm saying,\" said Olly.</p>\n<p>\"You might want to mind your voice in here,\" said Rose. \"We just got gifted territory from the Camarilla, and I'd kind of\nlike to keep it, if you don't mind.\"</p>\n<p>Lil Sis stared at her. \"Who'd you have to suck off to get territory from <em>them</em>?\"</p>\n<p>\"We gave someone a good donor.\"</p>\n<p>\"A good <em>boner</em>,\" said somebody, and that was the word that stuck.</p>\n<p>\"My stop's a little closer,\" said Lil Sis, when they came to it. \"Thanks for the ride. It won't happen again.\"</p>\n<h2>Barbara Hall Park</h2>\n<p>Rose parked on the street by the park, their park now, and checked her watch: an hour and a half before the start of\nsunup. \"Shall we scope it out? See what the feeding's like. Meet back here in an hour.\"</p>\n<p>She went off one way. That left Olly and Ethan and Ralph standing on the sidewalk.</p>\n<p>\"Guys,\" Olly said. \"I'm not normally caught up in these. I'm not normally just in the street shooting at people. You caught\nme at a weird time.\"</p>\n<p>\"Well,\" said Ralph, \"you sounded like you weren't super comfortable.\"</p>\n<p>\"That guy was invincible. Did you see that? I only shot him once.\"</p>\n<p>\"I've never seen anything like that,\" said Ralph.</p>\n<p>\"You haven't?\"</p>\n<p>\"Not exactly. I'd kind of figured out that we can't really be hurt easily. But getting shot in the face would probably hurt\nme quite a bit. I don't want to test it.\"</p>\n<p>\"Good policy,\" said Ethan.</p>\n<p>\"Ralph has the most to lose in the getting-shot-in-the-face department,\" Olly said. \"Handsome fellow that he is.\"</p>\n<p>\"That's the moneymaker.\"</p>\n<p>Olly kept worrying at it. \"It didn't seem like a good idea to go after the Sheriff, right? My friends, in that building.\nYou guys were inside that building. Seemed pretty secure.\"</p>\n<p>\"Going after the Sheriff,\" said Ethan, \"pretty fucking stupid, man. You attacked him and he saw your face.\"</p>\n<p>\"I would've killed him. But you could have killed him. You were right there.\"</p>\n<p>\"Yes. But I didn't attack him. I'm not worried about retaliation from him.\"</p>\n<p>\"You should be,\" said Ralph.</p>\n<p>\"I'm going to be honest,\" said Olly. \"I drove into him with a car. I thought that was going to handle the whole thing. The\ngun was like, if I needed a finishing blow. You ever hit a deer with your car?\"</p>\n<p>\"It's happened.\"</p>\n<p>\"Usually you've got to go out and finish the animal off. For ethical reasons. I shot that man in the head.\"</p>\n<hr />\n<p>In daylight, if any of them ever saw it in daylight, it was a neighbourhood of low buildings, two and three and four stories.\nShops at street level. Apartments over the shops. A few proper apartment buildings. Almost no houses.</p>\n<p>Ethan was already thinking about real estate. A building, or part of one, that he could lease. Move his work over here: it\nwas his business, he could do that. Staff there in the day, keeping an eye on things, and a ready explanation for the parts\nthat were hardened. <em>Why have you got a steel door and a whole bunch of non-lethal weapons lying around?</em> This is for work.\nIt's a legitimate business.</p>\n<p>He walked it with a notebook, taking down the numbers on the for-rent signs. One caught his eye: a door between two\nshopfronts, the kind that goes up to apartments, with a slot too narrow to reach through. Through the slot he could see\nstairs going up, and stairs going down. Commercial at street level, maybe residential above, maybe a basement below. That\nwould be perfect. He'd need somebody to make calls during business hours, and one of his people could do the walkthrough\nand hold up a phone for a video tour.</p>\n<p>Ralph looked at who lived here, because that was what a hunting ground was. The low apartments had grilles and grates on\ntheir windows, the kind that open from inside in a fire and not from outside. Getting in would be the question. But once in,\nthere was an embarrassment of riches: floor on floor of people asleep. He preferred his own ground. This would do as a\nbackup.</p>\n<p>Olly went up. He did his urban jumping to the top of a short building and looked out at the city, mournfully, and called\nTrieste, and explained that they'd lost Malik.</p>\n<p>\"I showed up and everyone was gone,\" she said. \"Where are you? Where's Lil Sis?\"</p>\n<p>\"Lil Sis is with us. It got really hot, and the Sheriff went inside a building, and some random person offered us a ride.\nLike, a vampire. So we took it.\"</p>\n<p>\"You just accept rides from strangers?\"</p>\n<p>\"It was a trying situation.\"</p>\n<p>\"All right. What's the plan with Malik? Is he dead?\"</p>\n<p>\"Word from Rami is <em>lab rats</em>. So we better hope he's dead, because the alternatives are worse. On the bright side, last time\nI saw him he was on fire.\"</p>\n<p>\"But you're okay. Lil Sis is okay.\"</p>\n<p>\"Yeah. We got out. I'm at this park. In Church–Wellesley.\"</p>\n<p>\"I'll be right over.\"</p>\n<hr />\n<p>She found Ralph first, checking the grates on a basement window. She kicked him gently behind the knee so his leg buckled a\nlittle.</p>\n<p>\"Are you the one that picked up my boy?\"</p>\n<p>\"Depends who your boy is.\"</p>\n<p>\"Mousy looking. Dark hair, kind of long. Olly. Likes to shoot people.\"</p>\n<p>\"Oh,\" said Ralph, \"is he shooting people now?\"</p>\n<p>\"Apparently.\"</p>\n<p>\"That's fun. Can you lead the way? By the way, I'm Ralph.\"</p>\n<p>\"Ralph.\" She shook his hand. \"How do you like your nights?\"</p>\n<p>\"Don't know yet. I'm kind of new to this.\"</p>\n<p>\"How do you like your sunrises? You should join us for one.\"</p>\n<p>\"For a sunrise? Seems painful.\"</p>\n<p>\"Most of us make it back.\"</p>\n<p>\"Not a fan of Russian roulette.\"</p>\n<p>\"Well, if you ever change your mind,\" said Trieste, \"it's the thrill of a lifetime.\"</p>\n<p>\"That,\" said Ralph, \"is what I'm worried about.\"</p>\n<p>Olly came down by the stairs. Climbing up a building was one thing; climbing down one sounded awful, and nobody locks the\nroof door from the inside anyway. He found the two of them just as Ralph was declining.</p>\n<p>\"There's the man of the hour,\" said Trieste. \"I heard you were shooting people now.\"</p>\n<p>\"It seemed like the situation called for it. I also hit him with a car.\"</p>\n<p>\"Before or after you shot him?\"</p>\n<p>\"Right before.\"</p>\n<p>\"The old one-two.\" She nodded. \"Wasn't sufficient, it sounds like.\"</p>\n<p>\"He didn't mind getting shot in the head. Or set on fire. Lil Sis seemed to mind-control him for a second. Tough guy.\"</p>\n<h2>King Dre</h2>\n<p>\"So we're not really on speaking terms,\" said Trieste, walking, \"but if anyone can throw down the Sheriff, it's King Dre, over\nin Scarborough. I bet we could get him to lend us a few heavies. Set up a trap, or come straight at the Sheriff. We can pay\nblood for blood. And we totally should.\"</p>\n<p>She said all of this with Ralph standing right there, a man she had met about four minutes ago. Congratulations, Ralph:\nco-conspirator.</p>\n<p>\"I heard the Prince wouldn't take kindly,\" said Ralph, mildly.</p>\n<p>\"Are you scared of the Prince?\"</p>\n<p>\"I haven't had the pleasure of meeting the Prince.\"</p>\n<p>\"I think we should talk to King Dredd,\" said Olly.</p>\n<p>\"Dre.\"</p>\n<p>\"I'm sure he won't object to being called King Dredd.\"</p>\n<p>Ethan heard the tail end of it, walking up. <em>Are you scared of the Prince?</em> His eyebrows went up a little. That sounded like a\nconversation worth listening to, so he listened. Ralph, beside him, said very little. Smile and nod. Get some heavies, start a\nwar. He was just being a sponge.</p>\n<p>Trieste talked, and dropped names, and Olly listened to her the way he'd learned to listen to her.</p>\n<p>The first thing he understood was that the space of things was bigger than he'd realized. Not the number of Kindred; there\nweren't that many Kindred in Toronto. Fewer, all told, than a high school. But there were ghouls, and human sympathizers, and\nother things that turned up here and there, and it all mattered out of proportion, because you didn't meet many people like\nyou in a night. They were scattered. They were apex predators. There's a reason you don't see bears going round in herds.</p>\n<p>The second thing he understood was that he couldn't tell how much of what she was saying was good. She talked like she knew\neverybody. Someone connected with a church of Caine. King Dre. A crew in East York that had fought the Sheriff before, who\ndidn't like talking to her or to the Sunburners but who she hoped to pull in. And Father Vivek, further north and east, who was\nmore tolerant of them, only they'd had a falling-out, and making it up would mean something like an apology, which was a hard\nsell. She said all of it the same way: <em>yes, I know these people, I'm very connected.</em> Some of it, Olly was sure, was made up, or\nwas someone she'd met once and called a friend, or was an easy sell that wouldn't be easy at all. He just couldn't tell which\nparts.</p>\n<p>Ethan, listening, could tell more. The names mostly checked out: people and places he'd heard of, or seen, or had from Rose. It\nwas the connections that didn't. The intentions, the capabilities. And the ones Rose had always seemed closest to, Evelyn and\nthe others out west, Trieste didn't mention at all.</p>\n<p>\"Here's an idea,\" said Olly. \"Our new friends Ralph and Ethan here actually have an in with the people the Sheriff's meeting.\nThey were just in that building. I think they're owed a favour. Maybe we pull some levers there. See if we can get Malik out.\nMight be the easiest way through.\"</p>\n<p>Trieste stopped. \"Wait. You guys were <em>working</em> with them?\"</p>\n<p>\"I don't think <em>working with</em> is the right phrasing,\" said Ralph. \"Dealing with is different from working with. And I understand\nyou want to save your friend. Malik, is it? His plan, in an immediate confrontation, was to set himself and another person on\nfire. That was his go-to, under stress. I don't think rescuing him is in his best interests long-term. And certainly not in\nyours.\"</p>\n<p>He wasn't lying. That was what made it work.</p>\n<p>Trieste looked at him, dumbfounded. \"So you're telling me you walked in, and met <em>Dominik</em>, and walked out.\"</p>\n<p>\"Yes.\"</p>\n<p>\"And walked out with <em>this</em> territory. Jesus. What did you give him?\"</p>\n<p>\"A knick-knack.\"</p>\n<p>\"A boner,\" said Olly. \"I heard.\"</p>\n<p>\"<em>Boner</em> was the word,\" Ralph agreed.</p>\n<p>She was too stunned to say anything for a moment. \"I guess we'll see,\" she said finally. \"So this is yours. Fuck me.\" Then:\n\"Can we play here sometimes?\"</p>\n<p>\"As long as you play nice.\"</p>\n<p>\"We can play nice. No gunfire.\"</p>\n<p>\"No fires. Don't start none.\"</p>\n<p>\"That was a one-time thing. Do you know where Lil Sis is?\"</p>\n<p>\"In the car,\" said Olly.</p>\n<p>\"I'll go fetch her. See you in a couple of nights. I'll let you know if we learn anything else about the Sheriff.\" She nodded\nat Ralph. \"Good to meet you, Ralph.\" And at Ethan, for about half a second: \"Ethan.\"</p>\n<p>\"Nice to meet you,\" said Ethan, to her back.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Rose came back shortly after. \"Find anything you like?\"</p>\n<p>\"Got my eye on a property,\" said Ethan. \"But I need to talk in private, when you have a minute.\"</p>\n<p>He told her what he'd heard. Trieste, King Dre, heavies, a trap for the Sheriff. \"What do you think, boss?\"</p>\n<p>\"I don't fancy their chances,\" said Rose. \"I'm not a fan of selling folks out to the Camarilla. But there could be a major boon in\nit.\"</p>\n<p>\"I definitely don't want to fight that guy.\"</p>\n<p>\"Oh, I'm not touching it with a ten-foot pole. They're not going to do it here, right?\"</p>\n<p>\"They said they'd play nice.\"</p>\n<p>\"Then deny all knowledge. Go about your life. If something happens and they get themselves killed, that's on them.\"</p>\n<p>They folded back into the others. Rose turned to Olly.</p>\n<p>\"You were at that boiler house. Todd's.\"</p>\n<p>\"Yeah.\"</p>\n<p>\"And you stole all their shit.\"</p>\n<p>\"Repossessed. Yes.\"</p>\n<p>\"It was yours originally, you could say.\" She considered him. \"So you're in the trade around here. What are you trying to do?\nMake? Distribute? Sell?\"</p>\n<p>\"I'm just trying to get the lay of the land. Not long ago I was a daylight guy. I knew a lot about the scene in Toronto, then. I'm\nstill trying to get a sense of who's buying and selling. But I'm interested in getting involved.\"</p>\n<p>\"Then I advise being extremely careful where you do what. Which was the problem with your friend Todd, who did not heed repeated\nadvisories after multiple infractions.\" She shrugged. \"But if you're looking to make your way, the real money is in artifacts.\nBooks. The occult shit. Anybody can make potions at this point.\"</p>\n<p>\"Can they,\" said Olly, who had been learning to make potions.</p>\n<p>\"Valuable to who? It's helpful if you're thin-blooded.\" She glanced at Ralph and Ethan. \"Nothing against thin-bloods. I have no\nbeef with you whatsoever. But factually, you don't tend to have much in the way of money or boons to offer. So the economy in\nselling to you kind of sucks. Stuff with real occult import, though. Now you've got real buyers. Real power. Real boons.\"</p>\n<p>\"So I'm a Walter White type,\" said Olly, \"and you're an Indiana Jones. I feel like we walked onto the wrong movie set.\"</p>\n<p>\"I'm not going to take any offense,\" said Ralph. \"As you said, none was meant. So clearly there's none for me to take.\"</p>\n<p>She called it a night, and drove them each home: Ralph to the market where he'd left his car, Olly to his place, Ethan last, to\nthe bail bondsman's. \"I've got business for the next few nights,\" she said to him. \"I'll be back by the weekend. You know how to\nreach me. I just won't be around.\"</p>\n<p>\"I've got a lead on a place,\" Ethan said. \"Might make a good base of operations. My people can make some calls and go by. It looks\ngood from outside, but that's only half the story.\"</p>\n<p>\"Let me know how it goes.\"</p>\n<h2>Two or Three Nights</h2>\n<p>Bill, the roommate, said hi when Ralph got home. He was still up, for some reason.</p>\n<p>Ralph took some of his money out and rented an apartment in the new neighbourhood, a second place, in a building with eleven other\nunits, which meant building access, which meant floors of them. He'd work out how to use that. He'd work out how to turn Rose's\ncontrol of this neighbourhood into <em>his</em> control of this neighbourhood, and that into something worth more than five million dollars,\nor the trade had been a bad one. It would take time. He had time.</p>\n<p>And he needed to stop being thin-blooded. He needed to be a real vampire. He'd have asked for a do-over on the Embrace, if there were\nanyone to ask. There wasn't, so he'd find out how it worked himself: he was on the bottom rung, and hungry to climb, literally. The\nCOAG was the only place he knew where Kindred gathered. He'd start there, whenever it was next, and leapfrog from whoever he met.</p>\n<p>As it turned out, he met someone before that. On the street, one night: a face he remembered from the tunnel. He ran to catch them\nup and introduced himself again, and they were brusque and tried to brush him off, and he kept asking. How do I find out when these\nthings happen? Where? Eventually they got a little pissy and gave him something. <em>The next Elysium's in a week. At such-and-such a\nhotel. Just make sure you bring a gift.</em></p>\n<p>It was right at the end that he realized. Either it wasn't true or it was a setup. He was sure it was one or the other; he couldn't\nhave told you which. Either way it was deeply disrespectful. And the thing inside him that he'd been keeping a lid on said: <em>fuck these\nguys.</em></p>\n<p>He wasn't a physical guy. He didn't want to burn a bridge he might need. So somebody else would have to suffer for it.</p>\n<p>There was a man waiting for a bus. Ralph hit him across the face and kept hitting him. Halfway through, with the man struggling a\nlittle, not much, it occurred to Ralph that this was an opportunity to feed, and he fed. It was a lot more brutal than he usually was.\nNobody was right beside them. There was somebody across the street.</p>\n<p>When his head cleared he knew there'd be people coming, police or paramedics, soon. He ran into the night. He was only a couple of blocks\nfrom the new apartment. On the way back it occurred to him that he should be more careful about shitting where he lived and ate.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Ethan's employee brought back a video of the place. The basement was completely below ground: no windows at all, a big selling point for\nanyone who dies in sunlight, and the walls down there would be brick or cinderblock against the earth. A place for himself, maybe a bunk\nor two, that could be made secure. Somewhere for a holding cell, for the actual work. The ground floor would be the office. The floor\nabove, storage, and the less public-facing rooms.</p>\n<p>It looked like a good location. By a couple of nights later he didn't have the lease yet, but he was sure: this was the place. The paperwork\nwas grinding. He'd started looking at what else was in the area, and what it meant for advertising.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Olly woke the next evening thinking about Trieste.</p>\n<p>He'd been going along with her. The rooftops, the sunrises. Tonight she'd been ready to throw her people at the Sheriff, and it had got\none of them taken, and made Olly wonder about her judgement for the first time. And he'd seen the scale of it now: the personal power,\na man who could take a bullet to the head, and the structure over that, the kind that could just <em>take</em> people. It bothered him. He didn't\nunderstand it. He didn't think it was right that people disappeared. He wanted to know more, and from where he stood, he couldn't know\nanything, or do anything about any of it.</p>\n<p>He was hungry. He was pissed off about the Sheriff. He didn't want to call Trieste.</p>\n<p>There was an abandoned shopping centre he knew. He posted up inside it and called the police, and waited.</p>\n<p>Two came first, and stood in the open and talked. Then two more, and they split up into pairs and moved around, and Olly moved around\ntoo, quietly, doing whatever he had to do to get them to separate. It took time. Eventually one of them went down a little side corridor\nalone, her partner just on the other side of a wall, the two of them calling out to each other as they looked around. She had a flashlight\nand a gun.</p>\n<p>He came at her, and she shot him, point blank, the way anyone would, in that terror.</p>\n<p>He didn't blink. He knew he'd been shot. It was like one of those mind-over-matter things where someone trains themselves not to feel what\ntheir body is feeling. <em>I can ignore that. The Sheriff ignored that.</em> He took hold of her and fed.</p>\n<p>Her partner found her later on the second floor, recovering, disoriented. Somewhere else, Olly dug the bullet out of himself. A little\ndiscomfort. He felt fine.</p>\n<p>It gave him an inflated sense of his own invulnerability. He did not know that it might not work like that next time.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>He put his ear to the ground after that, and heard about a disturbance two nights after the thing with the Sheriff. The rumours were\ninconsistent to the point of incoherence. But something had come to St. Joseph's, where the chantry was, where he'd gone up against the\nSheriff: some kind of nightmarish creature that had come through and terrorized the place.</p>\n<p>Maybe those other guys had heard something.</p>\n<p>He didn't know where exactly they'd be, only the general area. So one night he went to the park and sat on a bench and was conspicuous\nand killed time until one of them noticed.</p>\n<p>\"Mr. Olly,\" said Ralph. \"What brings you around?\"</p>\n<p>\"Ralph. How are the new digs?\"</p>\n<p>\"Wonderful.\"</p>\n<p>\"I heard about something. You guys seem pretty plugged in, especially in this area. I heard some sort of monster came through. Something.\nAround the school. St. Joseph's.\"</p>\n<p>\"No. What kind of monster?\"</p>\n<p>\"A few nights ago. Last night, maybe.\" He tried to remember how it had been put to him. \"Something menacing and strange. Conflicting\ndescriptions. Some sort of huge body-horror thing. Real nasty. Cronenberg. People saw it.\"</p>\n<p>\"You'd know if you'd heard about it, I guess,\" said Ralph. \"Must not have made much of a disturbance. Regardless of how disturbing it was.\"</p>\n<p>\"Maybe Ethan knows something. Is he around?\"</p>\n<p>\"He usually is. You can find him if you look.\"</p>\n<p>Ethan had called Rose earlier and not got through. What came back was a text: <em>Occupied. Stay where you are.</em> He'd answered with a\nthumbs-up.</p>\n<p>Now his phone buzzed again. Rose.</p>\n<p><em>Are you in the area? I need to come pick you up.</em></p>\n<p>He answered right away.</p>", "part": "Two", "played": "2025-12-02", "slug": "05-the-old-one-two", "title": "The Old One-Two", "words": 6616}, {"html": "<h2>Shit Rolls Downhill</h2>\n<p>This is what they knew, by the time the year turned.</p>\n<p>They had lent the bone to the Tremere, and the Tremere had lost it. The chantry had been boarded and guarded\nand warded, and something had come through it anyway, as if none of that was there: something past anything\nthey understood about their own condition, something that turned people into door mantels. Dominik's ghoul\nhad been made into something else, and it had heard some kind of song. There had been water on the\ncarpet afterward, hot, and it wasn't anything chemical, and it wasn't only water. The bone was said to have\nsomething to do with the Antediluvians, whatever those were exactly. And now the people who had lost it\nexpected the coterie to get it back, and the territory they had only just been given hung on whether they\ncould.</p>\n<p>\"Isn't that what the powerful do,\" said Ralph. \"Make their problems your problems. It's really an act of\ngenerosity.\"</p>\n<p>\"It's another way of saying shit rolls downhill,\" said Olly.</p>\n<p>Ethan was moving his business into the new building, which meant paperwork. Incorporation, permits, the\nbuild-out. It didn't happen in a night. Ralph had the apartment, a few blocks away. Olly had his notes on\nfleshcrafting, and a monster he wanted to know more about, and Trieste, who wanted to get into the chantry and\nget Malik out.</p>\n<p>Then Trieste texted him. King Dre wasn't interested in helping. She didn't seem inclined to try anything else\nabout Malik. She and the gang, though, had <em>hit the jackpot</em>. He should bring his friends by some time.</p>\n<p><em>Sure</em>, he wrote back. <em>If you really hit it off with those guys, you want me to bring them?</em></p>\n<p><em>I think they'd be very interested. Don't worry, we'll meet at night.</em> And, when he pushed: <em>Many hands make\nlight work.</em> Nothing else.</p>\n<p>He knew how it would look. He'd met these two on a fool's errand for Trieste, and they obviously thought she\nwas crazy, and now he had to drag them to something else of hers. It was like babysitting somebody who's\nvery drunk at a party: <em>please don't let this reflect too badly on me</em>. But he was more or less her dog. So\nhe went to the park, and found them, and made the pitch.</p>\n<p>\"You remember my friend Trieste. She's recently stumbled into something I think could be really useful for\nthe investigation. I don't really know what it is. But she knows I'm busy with this, so it must be relevant.\nIf she says it's urgent, we should check it out. She wants us to swing by tonight.\"</p>\n<p>\"The girl who likes to look at the sun?\" said Ralph.</p>\n<p>\"This is more of a two in the morning thing. We're not going to see the sun. It won't come up.\"</p>\n<p>\"I mean, that's insane,\" said Ethan. \"But if she's survived it this long, she must know something.\"</p>\n<h2>The Pit</h2>\n<p>The address was in the Distillery District, down by the lake, or as near the lake as the expressway let\nyou get. Forty blocks or so from the park: a short drive in the middle of the night, a long walk. Ralph drove.</p>\n<p>On the way they compared what they had.</p>\n<p>Ralph had two leads on the bone. One was Jason Cho, who'd offered him five million for it, and that was a dead\nend; he knew it was a dead end, and brought it up anyway. The other was the two vampires in the alley. The one\nwho'd been doing the feeding had dropped the bone. Ralph had seen their faces. Then they'd disappeared.</p>\n<p>\"Vampire feeding on vampire,\" Ralph said. \"Diablerie. I didn't know that was a thing.\"</p>\n<p>Olly had known one thing about vampires feeding on vampires, which was that Rami sometimes fed on him, and that\nwas nothing like what Ralph was describing. What Ralph was describing sounded like what had been done to the husk\nin the drawer.</p>\n<p>So he told them about the body. The markings. He had pictures of the markings on his phone. He didn't have any\nof the face; it hadn't seemed important.</p>\n<p>\"Is the body still around?\"</p>\n<p>\"I had to break into a morgue. Maybe it's still there.\"</p>\n<p>Ethan's question was simpler. Who had the horsepower to roll through a place like the chantry the way that thing\nhad? It seemed like it would be really hard. That had to narrow the list.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>The Distillery District was distilleries, and light manufacturing, and alleys. They walked down one toward the\nwater, and ahead of them against the glow of the highway were three or four figures with shovels, standing\naround the kind of heap a truck dumps on a lot to be picked up later. A couple of hundred feet away. The figures\nhad seen the car's lights come and go.</p>\n<p>\"Hey!\" That was Lil Sis. \"Come over here. We could use an extra shoveller.\"</p>\n<p>It wasn't a heap. It was a grave, a makeshift one, and there were nine bodies in it.</p>\n<p>\"We bit off a little more than we could chew,\" said Trieste. One end was half filled in already. She nodded at\nthe other. \"Go ahead and finish bleeding out those last couple. They're dead, but they need to be bled. And then\ngive them a little vitae. So they can have a rough wake-up.\"</p>\n<p>Ralph had been reading. Anything about the occult he could get his hands on, including anything that might say\nwhat he was. And he remembered this: an idea some of them had about the Embrace. Everywhere else in Kindred\nsociety it was a thing you did rarely and carefully. You were an apex predator. You made one, or two, and you\ninvested everything in them, and you held on to power and structure. But there were some who believed the\nopposite. Make them like fish lay eggs. Spam it. Whoever survives was meant to. He had never known whether that\nwas anything more than a theory.</p>\n<p>It was more than a theory.</p>\n<p>Olly remembered something too. Trieste had said, a long time ago, that she'd woken up in a pit with her crew.\nAnd that the people who killed her hadn't made it. She had never said what she meant by that. Olly had assumed\nthey woke up hungry and killed the people who'd made them. Her crew, he thought, might include Lil Sis. Might have\nincluded Malik.</p>\n<p>He found a moment to tell the other two, quietly. There might be some danger when these ones woke up.</p>\n<p>\"No kidding,\" said Ralph. \"I remember what I was like when I first woke up.\"</p>\n<p>Olly counted. Trieste, Lil Sis, and Zuzu. Three of them, and three of the coterie. And nine in the pit.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>The last three bodies were very recently dead. Not yet drained.</p>\n<p>Something like that almost never happens. You can drink until the Hunger is simply gone, and nobody dies of it,\nbecause nobody is left to die. Ethan drank until he was full. So did Olly.\nRalph abstained.</p>\n<p>The second part, the vitae, none of them rushed to. Ethan knew more than the other two about what it meant. In\nthe Camarilla's city, making one of them without the Prince's permission was a capital crime. These people were\nvery clearly not operating within Camarilla guidelines. He met Ralph's eyes, and Olly's.</p>\n<p>Olly went over to Trieste instead. \"So. Are we having kids? What's going on here?\"</p>\n<p>\"Whichever ones survive the sun,\" she said, \"replenish our pack.\"</p>\n<p>\"Because you've been losing so many to standing out in the morning.\"</p>\n<p>\"There's been a gradual decline. But here's the thing. Did you know I stayed outside until eleven in the morning\nthe other week?\"</p>\n<p>\"On the rooftop?\"</p>\n<p>\"We found a way. You know how we snorted Jonah's ashes?\"</p>\n<p>\"I couldn't forget it.\"</p>\n<p>\"We saved some. We've been experimenting. If you cut it the right way, it really helps with the sunburn. So this\nis win-win. We get a few new recruits, and-or we get some raw material.\"</p>\n<p>\"Oh my God,\" said Olly. \"Where did you find these people?\"</p>\n<p>\"Zuzu found most of them, honestly. I think they were here for a bachelorette party.\" She shrugged. \"It's fine.\nThey're from Saskatchewan.\"</p>\n<p>\"You said this is how you woke up. In a ditch.\"</p>\n<p>\"Yeah. There were like fifteen of us.\"</p>\n<p>\"And the people who put you in that ditch?\"</p>\n<p>\"No idea. I didn't see them.\"</p>\n<p>Ralph had been hearing fragments of this. Nobody was trying to keep it from him. \"I think we should make\nourselves scarce,\" he said. \"This isn't a scene we want to be involved in.\"</p>\n<p>\"Why'd you want me to bring these guys?\" Olly asked Trieste.</p>\n<p>\"I thought they might be interested. Either in the sire part, or in getting some of the goods.\" She held out her\nhand. Three vials of what looked like ash. \"These are for you guys. Call it paying you back. Paying it forward.\nWhatever.\"</p>\n<p>\"Three of them. Wow. I appreciate it.\"</p>\n<p>She had one more, a bigger stoppered beaker, and she tapped it against his like glasses. \"To Jonah.\"</p>\n<p>\"Are you doing it now?\"</p>\n<p>\"No. I'm assuming we won't be together when you partake. So. In anticipation.\"</p>\n<p>\"Always the romantic.\" He looked at the pit. \"Well. I guess I should give blood to one of these kiddos and see\nwhat happens.\"</p>\n<p>He cut himself and did it, for one of them. Ethan and Ralph didn't.</p>\n<p>\"This is psycho shit,\" said Ralph.</p>\n<p>\"I think I'm going to stick around,\" Olly said. \"See what happens. You don't see this every day.\"</p>\n<p>Ethan was doing math. The risks. The benefits.</p>\n<p>\"We could probably use their help,\" said Olly, \"if we're going to take down whatever broke out of that chantry.\"</p>\n<p>\"We're trying to earn good graces with the establishment,\" said Ralph. \"Involvement in this sort of thing is\ncounter-indicated.\"</p>\n<p>\"And what's the establishment ever done for you?\" said Olly.</p>\n<p>\"They literally just gave us feeding grounds. Territory that could be ours.\"</p>\n<p>\"They also lost the valuable thing you loaned them.\"</p>\n<p>\"Which makes us valuable,\" said Ralph. \"Makes us useful.\"</p>\n<p>\"All right,\" said Olly. \"Fair enough. You were never here.\"</p>\n<h2>Sunup</h2>\n<p>Trieste and Lil Sis saw to the last of them, and they all filled in the grave. Then they sat back and somebody lit\na joint. It was that kind of conversation, after: old children's cartoons, the shitty jobs they'd had when they\nwere alive.</p>\n<p>Olly was comfortable. That surprised him a little. The sun-watching had alarmed him; it had looked borderline\nsuicidal. But making more of themselves to replace the ones they'd lost made sense. He didn't mind that it was\nillegal. It seemed like a good hang.</p>\n<p>Around four, he heard stirring in the dirt, and then there were hands coming up out of it.</p>\n<p>He remembered his own first waking. Nightmarish. Not knowing where you were, and a wanting in you rolling stronger\nthan anything you'd felt alive. Rami had been there. Rami had turned it somewhere else fast. But the first instant\nof it had been intense.</p>\n<p>These ones had nobody to turn it for them. It was pure feral energy. They came out of the ground clawing, over one\nanother, in whatever they'd died in, going for anything. The first to find its feet took Lil Sis's shovel full in the\nhead, swung like a baseball bat, and went down. Zuzu picked up another shovel and waited.</p>\n<p>Olly had not expected random violence against the vampire babies. He backed off.</p>\n<p>\"You want to find victims for them,\" Zuzu told him, matter-of-fact. \"But first you've got to keep them in place long\nenough to see if they have stamina. It's going to be a long hour or so till the sun comes up.\" She offered something.\n\"If you want to snort a little Jonah now, it'll buy us till seven or eight at least. More time than we'll need.\"</p>\n<p>\"I guess I'd better hit that. Aren't they going to be pissed when they finally get out, after you knock them in the\nhead all morning?\"</p>\n<p>\"It wears off surprisingly quickly,\" said Trieste. \"Once they're feeding.\" Then, as if it needed saying: \"It's not that\nthe Masquerade they always talk so big about is sacred. But we have some self-preservation instinct. We're not completely\nsuicidal. Nine fresh ravenous ones charging off onto the highway paints a target on our backs. So we funnel them where we\nwant them. The plant over there opens in twenty minutes anyway. There'll be fresh blood, easy.\"</p>\n<p>\"Have you done this before?\"</p>\n<p>\"We've done this before.\"</p>\n<p>\"And what happened to them?\"</p>\n<p>Zuzu shrugged. \"I turned out fine. We lost most of my litter. Tragic.\" Another shrug. \"They weren't cut out for it.\"</p>\n<p>What followed was an hour of batting them back, half-heartedly. They were confused, and disoriented, and they fell over one\nanother, and then one would find its footing and charge, and get a shovel in the face. Some of them got hurt in ways that\nwould heal, roughly. Somebody went over to the plant and came back with a shift worker, unconscious, and held him ready, but\ndidn't throw him in.</p>\n<p>Everyone took a little snort.</p>\n<p>The sun came up.</p>\n<p>All of them but one went to ash. Lil Sis tossed the shift worker to the one who was left, and it latched onto his neck and\nfed in a crazed stupor, while the rest of them went about carefully gathering up what was left of the other eight.</p>\n<p>Trieste and Lil Sis were contemporaries, Olly thought, watching them work. Both older than Zuzu. He helped where he could. He\ntried to be a good sport about it, and they gave him credit for that.</p>\n<p>\"I'll let you know when the next batch is available,\" Trieste said. \"And if you guys want to explore the sunlight, there's some\ngreat stuff that's open during the day.\"</p>\n<p>It had to be processed, she said, before it did what it did. A composite.</p>\n<p>\"Can I get a ride home? The guys I came with dipped.\"</p>\n<p>They didn't have a ride. There was one of those shitty electric scooters. Olly rode it home along the highway in full daylight,\non borrowed time, with a nostril full of Jonah.</p>\n<h2>Who Could Do That</h2>\n<p>Ethan was not going to crack a book. He was mostly literate. What he did was find people who knew things, starting with Rose, and\nask them who in this city was powerful enough to go through the chantry like that.</p>\n<p>A handful, it turned out.</p>\n<p>Evelyn Skye, the Baron of Kensington, who was Rose's own sire. Father Vivek, out in Riverdale, way northeast. King Dre, in\nScarborough. Someone named Raya, who claimed great powers without anyone having seen her demonstrate them: a Noddist priestess.\nEthan had heard the phrase in passing and paid it no mind. There was apparently a church of Caine, a vampire church, and she was\nthe local chapter's priestess. Elijah Kane, the Sheriff, who had shrugged off a car and a bullet in the space of a minute; he'd\nqualify. And the Seneschal, Maeva, who was known to be powerful and rumoured to be much more powerful than she let on.</p>\n<p>It was a longer list than Ethan would have guessed. And not one of them fit the description of what had been seen.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Ralph set up a drawing pad.</p>\n<p>He remembered very little, personally. Something had gone into his head in that alley and pushed; there might be holes in what\nhe had. So he didn't try to remember. He did a couple of practice runs with his eyes open, to get the feel of the pad's edges. Then\nhe closed his eyes, and ran what he had over and over in his mind, a few frames, almost stills, of the tall one turning toward him,\nand let it go straight down into his hand without trying to force it.</p>\n<p>What came out was humanoid, which was something: it wasn't just lines. No hair, or else the hair pulled back tight. Hands that were\nclawed, and not human hands. Ears that were not like an elf's but like a goblin's, some deformation of the body that he hadn't\nconsciously known he had seen. It looked alien. Monstrous before it looked like anything else. Whatever it had been born, the drawing\ncouldn't tell you: not a man or a woman, not from anywhere. A monstrous human first and foremost.</p>\n<p>It looked more like his sire than like any other Kindred he'd met. It wasn't his sire.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Olly sent proof of life to the group chat. <em>Hey guys, that was a lot weirder than I expected. But I did get this powder that can help\nwith sun immunity, so, happy to share. We should still connect soon about the monster that's loose in your neighbourhood. Again, sorry\nabout that. I really didn't know what we were walking into.</em></p>\n<p>Ralph wrote back. <em>Can you take me to where you found that body you mentioned?</em></p>\n<h2>The Morgue, Again</h2>\n<p>Olly texted Claude first, to find out if the body was supposed to still be there. Claude didn't text back.</p>\n<p>\"Well. I didn't get word. Do you want to go check it out? We'll have to sneak into the morgue.\"</p>\n<p>\"I'm very good at breaking and entering,\" said Ralph. \"It's like my second specialty.\"</p>\n<p>They went that night. Olly knew the way and went quiet, a quiet so complete that his hands made no sound on anything they touched, and\nRalph did his own version, and between them they eased doors open and waited out the people still moving around a hospital in the small\nhours, and came down to the drawer.</p>\n<p>It stuck. Not locked, just jammed. They wrenched it open. The body was there, as Olly had left it: a withered husk of one of them, with\nthe inscriptions, and the holes and ports in the skin where things had gone in.</p>\n<p>Ralph looked at it for a while. He was nearly certain. This was the one he'd seen pinned to the fence.</p>\n<p>\"We should take the body with us,\" Ralph said. \"For Masquerade reasons. I don't want the FBI getting involved. Or whoever it is up\nhere. Sorry. I just came back from California.\"</p>\n<p>\"I think it's secure here. I know somebody who works here.\"</p>\n<p>\"Are they in the know enough to cover it up?\"</p>\n<p>\"Is that really worth it to you? How would we even get it out?\"</p>\n<p>\"You've got the feet. I grab the shoulders, and we walk.\"</p>\n<p>\"I've never smuggled a body out of anywhere.\"</p>\n<p>\"You seemed pretty comfortable with bodies yesterday.\"</p>\n<p>\"I was surprised. It was the first time.\" Olly considered the drawer. \"First time for everything. All right. Let's get this guy out of\nhere.\"</p>\n<p>There was a body bag. Without any water in it the husk weighed maybe forty pounds, which was one mercy; they didn't need a gurney or lab\ncoats or anybody to talk to. They carried it out the way they had come in. There would be questions later, at the hospital: who were\nthose people, why was that drawer wrenched open, why was it empty, what had even been in it, and why didn't the logs agree. None of\nthose questions would have anybody to answer them.</p>\n<p>They put it in Ralph's trunk.</p>\n<p>In the clear, Olly explained to Ralph what an athanor corporis was. The body in the trunk had been one, in its unlife: a thing for\nstoring blood concoctions, making them. \"Like a vial,\" he said. \"A Kindred vial. For drugs, basically.\"</p>\n<p>He didn't say what else he knew about it.</p>\n<p>\"That's why I want to get in good with the establishment,\" said Ralph. He didn't say what he didn't want to end up as.\nIt was in his trunk.</p>\n<p>\"I'd hate that,\" said Olly.</p>\n<h2>The Marx Brothers</h2>\n<p>Ethan was at the office, doing admin, when Ralph came in with Olly behind him.</p>\n<p>\"Ethan, my man. How's it going? I've got a corpse in the car.\" Ralph smiled. \"I've got a plan. It's stupid. But I think it'd be\ngood.\"</p>\n<p>\"You're really instilling confidence.\"</p>\n<p>\"It's better than I'm selling it.\" It was simple. They had the victim of their prime suspect. They had a drawing of the suspect. They\nshould go to the chantry and give Dominik an update on his little quest. Maybe he'd recognize the picture. Maybe the magic vampires\ncould do something with a victim's body to track the one who'd done it. Or maybe they'd just be glad somebody had got a Kindred corpse\nout of human hands. \"Either way it makes us look good. It looks like we're making progress. It buys us time. We keep our turf a little\nlonger.\"</p>\n<p>\"Demonstrable progress,\" said Ethan. \"All right.\"</p>\n<p>He texted Rose. <em>We found this. We think it might be related. We were also doing a little Masquerade protection, as a bonus. We're\nthinking of going over to the chantry to give them the update. What do you think?</em></p>\n<p>The dots came up. Went away. Came back. Either she was writing a novel or she was writing and deleting, writing and deleting. What\ncame, eventually, was short.</p>\n<p><em>I've called ahead to let them know you're coming.</em></p>\n<p>\"Inspiring,\" said Ralph.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>The iron door had been repaired. You'd never know. If you looked very closely there were signs of recent work, but the whole thing had\nbeen cleaned and put right in a matter of days, and that told you something: somebody had paid a great deal of money to have very, very\ncompetent people drop whatever they were doing and do this first.</p>\n<p>They left the body in the back seat of Ralph's car under a jacket, and knocked.</p>\n<p>Dominik opened the door himself, and looked at them with contempt. \"Why are you here?\"</p>\n<p>\"Two things,\" said Ralph. \"A status update on our investigation, and on recovering the relic. And a semi-related matter. A body we've\nrecovered.\"</p>\n<p>Dominik didn't move out of the doorway. He waited.</p>\n<p>Ralph held up the printout. \"I believe this is our prime suspect.\"</p>\n<p>Dominik looked at it. \"I don't recognize them. Am I supposed to?\"</p>\n<p>\"I assumed you know many more people than I do. And these are very distinctive features. They might indicate a particular origin.\"</p>\n<p>Dominik's face said, very clearly, that his time was being wasted.</p>\n<p>\"The second thing. I have the body of another vampire. One this person diablerized. It was in a hospital morgue.\"</p>\n<p>\"You have it here?\"</p>\n<p>\"Yes.\"</p>\n<p>\"Bring it in.\"</p>\n<p>He went inside, and they could hear him talking to people, having things moved. All three of them went down to the car together and all\nthree came back up together. Nobody wanted to be the one left alone with Dominik.</p>\n<p>When they came back there was a large table with a heavy cloth over it, and plastic under the cloth, and instruments laid out, some of them\nsurgical and some of them ceremonial, a great many little blades and shears. They brought the bag all the way in. Dominik opened it, and his\nwhole manner changed.</p>\n<p>\"How did you find this?\"</p>\n<p>\"Through disreputable thin-blood contacts,\" said Ralph.</p>\n<p>\"Well. Occasionally you seem to have some worth.\"</p>\n<p>He went over the body with his hands, and they could feel him getting frustrated. He went into another room and came back with needles, and\ntried to draw something out of it, anything, and came up dry. He started asking questions and stopping halfway through them, deciding, it\nseemed, both whether they were worth asking and how much he wanted them to know. Were there fluids near the body? Was this everything that was\nthere when it was found? What had their contact said?</p>\n<p>\"I don't know where the body was recovered from,\" said Ralph. \"When I saw him diablerize this one, he wiped my memory, briefly, and dropped the\nbone. He took the body with him. It turned up somewhere else.\"</p>\n<p>\"And you don't know what he had—\" Dominik stopped. Started again. \"Do any of you have connections to a blood alchemist?\"</p>\n<p>\"Yes,\" said Ralph, who didn't, but was sure somebody did.</p>\n<p>\"There are various markings here. It's possible there's some kind of warding ritual inscribed upon the skin. But I suspect this one was involved\nin some of the more <em>disreputable</em>, as you say, alchemical concoctions we have been dealing with. If you could provide a referral. Ideally\nsomeone willing to come here and examine it under my observation. We could at least rule some things out.\"</p>\n<p>\"I'll see what we can do.\"</p>\n<p>Dominik was turning the head side to side. \"I didn't realize,\" he said, mostly to himself, \"that we had local Tzimisce.\"</p>\n<p>\"Can I ask what indicates Tzimisce?\" said Olly.</p>\n<p>Dominik looked at him the way you'd explain something to a very small child. He turned the head for them. There was a build-up of skin and\nmuscle that was simply not human: whole additional muscles. He drew the skin down at the collarbone, and there were ridges there, growing out\nof the bone. Not human. Not even what the Nosferatu tended to have.</p>\n<p>\"Protrusions like these are usually indicative of someone molding their own body into some other shape. It isn't unique to the Tzimisce. But\nit is very rarely seen outside them.\"</p>\n<p>\"So this Kindred was morphing himself.\"</p>\n<p>\"That would be my suspicion.\"</p>\n<p>\"Your ghoul got fleshcrafted,\" said Olly. \"And the Tzimisce are commonly associated with fleshcrafting. That's kind of an interesting\ncoincidence.\"</p>\n<p>\"I don't believe in coincidences.\"</p>\n<p>\"What would be the political dimensions? If there was a small group of Tzimisce in Toronto we didn't know about?\"</p>\n<p>\"In principle, no matter. They are under the Prince's authority as any others. Historically, they have the unfortunate tendency of aligning\nwith the Sabbat. And of disregarding the Masquerade and our traditions in their entirety. If they were to swear fealty, it would be no matter.\"\nHe looked down at the husk. \"But what happened to my ghoul strongly indicates another. Clearly not this one. Perhaps its sire. Very\ninteresting.\" He straightened. \"Unfortunately it is fully drained of any vitae that might have identified it. However. If your courtroom sketch\nis in fact the diablerist, we could determine some <em>very</em> interesting things through an examination of their vitae. We wouldn't even need to\nkill them right away.\"</p>\n<p>\"I appreciate that,\" said Ralph.</p>\n<p>\"I know I'll enjoy it. Anyway. Be on your way.\"</p>\n<p>\"We'll report again when we have substantive progress.\"</p>\n<p>\"Great to see you, Dominik!\" said Olly.</p>\n<p>Dominik did not respond to that.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>It had gone better than it had any right to. They'd asked him things and he'd answered, and they'd asked follow-ups, and he'd answered those\ntoo. Olly had a theory about why. The body had changed what they were worth to him, a little. What it had done to his curiosity was a lot more\nthan a little. And the Tzimisce part had surprised him; he'd been thinking it through out loud.</p>\n<p>And someone who seemed so deep in blood magic was looking outside for help with alchemy. That was interesting too. Maybe it was a corner of\nthings he knew less about.</p>\n<p>\"My sire's a blood alchemist,\" Olly said, \"if that's any use.\"</p>\n<p>\"I'm sure only thin-bloods know any,\" said Ralph. \"Rose probably knows a couple. If she doesn't, we'll find one.\" He was pleased. \"I think we've\ngone from the Three Stooges to the Marx Brothers, in his eyes.\"</p>\n<p>\"That's a promotion. Which one's Groucho?\"</p>\n<p>\"Me. Of course. I'm the leader.\"</p>\n<p>The part Olly kept turning over was different. They'd been thinking the one who killed this Kindred was the one who went through the chantry.\nNow it looked like it was someone who'd been <em>close</em> to the victim. A sire, maybe.</p>\n<p>\"So it's a one-Tzimisce problem,\" said Ralph. \"Or a three-Tzimisce problem.\"</p>\n<h2>Rami</h2>\n<p>Ralph kept both places. Most nights he slept at Bill's. The apartment was a show apartment, for bringing people back to. Seductive feeding.\nMeetings with vampires. That kind of thing.</p>\n<p>\"Those shovel-head people,\" he said to Olly, at some point. \"A bachelorette party, in its entirety. Not good people. And I'm not squeamish.\nSomeone has to die to become a vampire. Someone dies to feed us, or gets caught in the crossfire. That's the cost of things. But that seemed\nexcessive. Wasteful.\"</p>\n<p>\"I'm sure they were being very douchey,\" said Olly.</p>\n<p>The three of them went over the park, their ground, together. There was something under it: a line of something running through the earth, a\ncurrent, that each of them felt in their own way. Nobody gives up prime ground. Maybe it had been given to them for a reason.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Rami, when Olly came, didn't let him talk right away. He came close, and took Olly by the back of the neck, and fed. Not long. A top-off. Olly\nfelt himself get hungrier.</p>\n<p>\"Okay,\" said Rami. \"I can concentrate now. What's going on?\"</p>\n<p>\"You've been eagerly waiting for an update on the body. And I've stumbled into a lot of information. I know somebody who witnessed the murder of\nthat corpse. It definitely was an athanor. And we took it to Dominik, at the chantry. He had a closer look, and he thought a blood alchemist\nmight be able to give a better assessment of what happened to it. So I thought of you. I didn't mention your name.\"</p>\n<p>\"I'm sorry. You went to Dominik. At the <em>chantry</em>.\"</p>\n<p>\"Do you remember hearing about this abomination downtown, wreaking havoc?\"</p>\n<p>\"I don't keep up with the news.\"</p>\n<p>\"I'm looking into it for him.\"</p>\n<p>\"Sit down.\"</p>\n<p>He pointed at his work chair, and started hooking Olly up, and ran him through a cycle: flushing the vitae out of him, straining something out of\nit, putting something else in, and flushing it back. When it was over Olly's blood ran sanguine. What was in him now, Rami said, was much less\nvaluable, more neutral. There was no hurry for it.</p>\n<p>Olly understood, slowly, what had just happened. What had been cooking in him had been worth a great deal. When Rami heard <em>Dominik</em>, the first\nthing he'd thought was how much Dominik would love to have it. And he had panicked.</p>\n<p>\"Do you think Kindred can parse what's inside me? From interacting with me?\"</p>\n<p>\"I don't know of a means to do that. I don't know what some of these blood-magic powers can and can't do. I'd be surprised if they could tell\nthe qualities of what you had brewing at a given time. But they don't need to, if they know that you are an athanor. All they need to do is tap\nyou, and experiment.\"</p>\n<p>\"But I'm not walking around with a sign on my back. There's no way to tell by looking at me.\"</p>\n<p>\"Not looking at you as you are, no. But again. I don't know what means he has.\" Rami sat back. \"I think we have been extraordinarily lucky.\"</p>\n<p>\"Have you met this guy? He's imposing.\"</p>\n<p>\"I have avoided meeting him.\"</p>\n<p>\"Do you want to meet him? A boon from him is worth a lot.\"</p>\n<p>\"I will meet him once,\" said Rami. \"So. Let me know what I need to bring, and when. And I will be there.\"</p>\n<p>What Rami did not need to say was that Dominik must never know he was Olly's sire. Olly was thin-blooded, and unregistered, and he had been made\nwithout anyone's permission. Rami was not thin-blooded. But he did not like being governed. He had avoided the Barons, and avoided the Camarilla,\nand done his own thing in private. Even in the blood-alchemy scene he kept to himself, which was part of why he had never come up on Rose's radar as a\nproblem. He had enormous contempt for the ones who played around irresponsibly, or who got addicted to strange things. He had standards and he kept\nto them, in his lane, and paid no attention to what anyone did on either side of it. It wasn't, Olly thought, a very coherent worldview.</p>\n<h2>Distillation</h2>\n<p>Rose came by to see the new place, and Ethan showed her around. The business setup. The holding cells going in, over here. And somewhere in the\ntour he slipped in the body, and Dominik, and how it had gone, because keeping her up to date was what he did.</p>\n<p>\"That's partly why I came by,\" said Rose. \"He's been very active, in some of the networks I'm active in. Looking for some very specific things.\nYou and I have confiscated a fair amount of paraphernalia over the last seven or eight months. Some of it might be very useful to him. I didn't want\nto duplicate efforts. I want maximum leverage. How much competition do you think we have? Can we be his exclusive supplier, on the alchemy side?\"</p>\n<p>\"There's some competition. But being exclusive might work. The others won't be super excited about working with the Camarilla.\"</p>\n<p>\"Good. And the thing he's really been looking for, the thing that seems to be incredibly scarce, is somebody who can distill things in their body.\nHave you ever heard of that? Do you know anybody who can do that?\"</p>\n<p>\"How would you know somebody could? Unless you tried it on them, and it failed, or it worked?\"</p>\n<p>\"Let's just say there are some subreddits that are a little loose with their words. I have a general idea how to get it working. If you can find me a\nsubject, I'll see what I can do.\" She looked around the building. \"He's paying very well. We might be able to get a permanent lock on this place.\nAnd an upgrade.\"</p>\n<p>\"I'll never complain about that.\"</p>\n<hr />\n<p>When the three of them next sat down together, Olly had news: his blood-alchemy friend would meet Dominik and look at the body.</p>\n<p>Ethan had news too. \"New project from Rose. We need to find a—\" He hunted for the word. He'd heard it recently. \"An athanor corporis. As I understand\nit, somebody who holds blood, for alchemy.\"</p>\n<p>Olly jumped like he'd put his hand on a hot stove.</p>\n<p>\"That's just crazy that you bring that up,\" he said, \"because that body we found was one. You know.\"</p>\n<p>What he said was straight-faced enough, and plausible enough, that Ralph mentally checked the box. <em>Yep. Checks out.</em></p>\n<p>Ethan was watching his eyes, and saw sheer panic in them.</p>\n<p>It told him one of two things. Either Olly could do it himself. Or Olly knew somebody who could, and did not want that discovered at all: somebody\nhe was very close to, or somebody very valuable, held very close to the chest. Ethan didn't know Olly well enough to know which.</p>\n<p>They set it up the only way they could. Rose would talk to Dominik. Dominik would name a night. And Rami would come to the chantry, once.</p>", "part": "Two", "played": "2026-01-06", "slug": "07-the-litter", "title": "The Litter", "words": 5842}], "city": [], "coterie": [], "home": null, "people": [], "trade": []};
